window.MLP_QBANK=[{"id": "fundamentals-90ea0ca", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Explain the bias–variance trade-off.", "a": "<p>Expected test error = bias² + variance + irreducible noise. Bias is error from overly simple assumptions (underfitting); variance is error from sensitivity to the specific training sample (overfitting). Increasing model complexity typically lowers bias and raises variance; the goal is the complexity that minimises total error, found via validation. Regularisation, more data and ensembling shift the trade-off.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-1155af6", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "How do you detect and prevent overfitting?", "a": "<p>Detect: training performance much better than validation/CV performance; learning curves with a persistent gap. Prevent: more data or augmentation, simpler model, regularisation (L1/L2, dropout), early stopping, feature selection, ensembling (bagging), proper CV for tuning, and removing leakage.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-91bf57e", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?", "a": "<p>Supervised learning trains on labelled examples to predict a target (classification/regression) and is evaluated against true labels. Unsupervised learning has no labels and finds structure — clusters, low-dimensional representations, density/anomalies — and evaluation is harder (internal metrics, downstream usefulness).</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-e9ef60e", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Parametric vs non-parametric models?", "a": "<p>Parametric models have a fixed number of parameters and strong functional assumptions (linear regression, logistic regression, Naive Bayes) — efficient, need less data, may underfit. Non-parametric models grow in complexity with data (kNN, trees, kernel methods, GPs) — flexible, need more data, risk overfitting, often slower at prediction.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-e8735e3", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Generative vs discriminative models?", "a": "<p>Generative models learn P(x|y) and P(y) (hence the joint) and use Bayes' rule to classify; they can generate samples and handle missing data (Naive Bayes, GMM, HMM). Discriminative models learn P(y|x) or the boundary directly (logistic regression, SVM, trees). Discriminative models usually have lower asymptotic error; generative ones can be better with little data.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-c235d81", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why do we need a separate validation and test set?", "a": "<p>The validation set (or CV) is used repeatedly for model selection and hyperparameter tuning, so performance on it is optimistically biased. The test set is touched once at the end to give an unbiased estimate of generalisation. Reusing the test set for decisions turns it into another validation set.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-4463765", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the curse of dimensionality?", "a": "<p>As features grow, volume grows exponentially, so data become sparse, distances between points concentrate (nearest ≈ farthest), and the amount of data needed to generalise grows exponentially. Distance-based methods suffer most. Remedies: feature selection, dimensionality reduction, regularisation, domain knowledge.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-5d54fd4", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the difference between a loss function and an evaluation metric?", "a": "<p>The loss is optimised during training and needs mathematical convenience (differentiable, convex where possible) — e.g. log-loss. The metric measures what the business cares about and may be non-differentiable — e.g. F1, precision@k, revenue. Choose a loss aligned with the metric and select thresholds/hyperparameters on the metric.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-f7b2825", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Batch vs stochastic vs mini-batch gradient descent?", "a": "<p>Batch uses the full dataset per update: accurate gradients, expensive, smooth convergence. Stochastic uses one sample: cheap and noisy, can escape shallow local minima, needs decaying learning rates. Mini-batch (32–1024) balances both and exploits vectorised hardware — the default in practice.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-d79113c", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is the relationship between MLE, MAP and regularisation?", "a": "<p>MLE maximises the likelihood. MAP maximises likelihood × prior; taking logs adds −log prior to the loss. A zero-mean Gaussian prior adds an L2 penalty (Ridge/weight decay); a Laplace prior adds an L1 penalty (Lasso). So regularisation = encoding a prior belief that parameters are small.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-9257f25", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What would you do if your model performs well on training data but poorly in production?", "a": "<p>Check for data leakage in training features; train/serve skew (different preprocessing or feature computation online); distribution shift (data/concept drift) between training period and now; a validation scheme that didn't mirror production (random split vs time split, unseen groups); label definition differences; and bugs in the serving pipeline. Then fix the root cause and set up monitoring.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-6ef435d", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you approach a new ML problem?", "a": "<p>Clarify the business goal and how predictions will be used; define target, metric and baseline; check data availability, quality and leakage; choose a validation scheme mirroring production; build a simple baseline; iterate on features and models with error analysis; evaluate on slices; consider deployment constraints and monitoring; communicate trade-offs.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-d16bc92", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the no free lunch theorem and what does it mean in practice?", "a": "<p>Averaged over all possible data-generating distributions, every learning algorithm performs equally. A model is only better when its inductive bias matches the problem. Practically: no universally best model — try several reasonable ones and validate on your data.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-2bc1a48", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "Can a model have both high bias and high variance?", "a": "<p>Yes — e.g. a model with the wrong functional form (bias) that's also fit on tiny noisy data with many parameters (variance), or a deep tree on badly chosen features. The decomposition terms aren't strictly opposed; good features can reduce both.</p>"}, {"id": "fundamentals-7e8ea74", "p": "fundamentals.html", "g": "ML fundamentals", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is inductive bias?", "a": "<p>The set of assumptions a learning algorithm uses to generalise beyond seen data: linearity (linear models), smoothness/locality (kNN, RBF), axis-aligned piecewise-constant structure (trees), translation equivariance (CNNs), feature independence (Naive Bayes). Choosing a model is choosing an inductive bias.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-f6d1b01", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "How do you handle missing values?", "a": "<p>First understand why they're missing (MCAR/MAR/MNAR) and how much. Options: drop rows/columns if few/mostly missing; simple imputation (median/mode) plus a missing-indicator flag; model-based (KNN, iterative/MICE); time-series fill (forward-fill/interpolate); or models that handle NaN natively (gradient boosting). Fit imputers on training data only, and validate the choice with CV.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-6e19781", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Explain MCAR, MAR and MNAR.", "a": "<p>MCAR: missingness is unrelated to any data — deleting is unbiased. MAR: missingness depends on observed variables — imputation conditioning on them works. MNAR: missingness depends on the unobserved value itself (e.g. high incomes withheld) — any method is biased without modelling the mechanism; missing indicators help capture it.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-a1a0a79", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "When should you normalise vs standardise?", "a": "<p>Standardise (z-score) as the general default for linear models, SVM, PCA, kNN, neural nets — it centres and handles unbounded data. Min-max normalise when you need a bounded range (e.g. pixel intensities, some NN inputs) and there are no strong outliers. RobustScaler with outliers. Trees need neither.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-d78cf63", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "One-hot vs label encoding — when to use each?", "a": "<p>One-hot for nominal categories in linear/distance-based models (no false order). Label/ordinal encoding for ordinal categories, or for tree models where arbitrary integer codes are acceptable. For high cardinality, use target/frequency/hashing encoding or native categorical support.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-8695a47", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is target encoding and its risk?", "a": "<p>Replacing each category with the (smoothed) mean of the target for that category. Risk: target leakage — a row's own label influences its feature, and rare categories get extreme values that memorise labels. Mitigate with out-of-fold (cross-fitted) encoding, smoothing toward the global mean, and adding noise.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-7a02ed6", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is data leakage? Give examples.", "a": "<p>When training uses information that won't be available at prediction time, inflating offline metrics. Examples: features recorded after the outcome (target leakage), fitting scalers/imputers/feature selection/target encoders on all data before splitting, random splits on time series, the same customer in train and test, duplicates across splits.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-9cd9df7", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you deal with an imbalanced dataset?", "a": "<p>Choose suitable metrics (PR-AUC, F1, recall@precision, cost); stratify splits; apply class weights or resampling (undersampling, SMOTE) only on training folds; tune the decision threshold on validation; consider ensemble methods like balanced random forests; get more minority data; recalibrate probabilities if needed.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-71f2605", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How does SMOTE work and what are its drawbacks?", "a": "<p>For each minority sample, pick one of its k nearest minority neighbours and create a synthetic point at a random position on the line between them. Drawbacks: can create noisy/overlapping samples in class-overlap regions, ignores the majority class distribution, struggles in high dimensions and with categorical features (use SMOTENC), and must be applied only inside training folds. Often class weights + threshold tuning do as well.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-c66c6c8", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Filter vs wrapper vs embedded feature selection?", "a": "<p>Filter: rank features by statistics independent of the model (correlation, chi², mutual information) — fast but ignores interactions. Wrapper: search subsets by training models (RFE, forward/backward) — accounts for interactions, expensive, overfit-prone. Embedded: selection during training (Lasso, tree importances) — efficient compromise.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-60dc24f", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you detect and treat outliers?", "a": "<p>Detect with visualisation, z-scores, IQR rule, robust z (MAD), or multivariate methods (Isolation Forest, Mahalanobis). Investigate cause: data error (fix/remove), legitimate extreme (keep, maybe transform/cap), or the signal itself (fraud). Treatments: winsorising, log transforms, robust scaling, robust losses, tree models.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-5b8c62c", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How would you encode a feature with 10,000 categories?", "a": "<p>Target encoding with cross-fitting and smoothing, frequency encoding, hashing trick, grouping rare categories into “Other”, learned embeddings (neural nets), or native categorical support in LightGBM/CatBoost. Avoid one-hot (sparse and huge) unless using a linear model on sparse matrices.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-6cbb766", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why should preprocessing be inside a pipeline?", "a": "<p>So every transformation is fit only on training folds during CV and applied identically at inference — preventing leakage and train/serve skew — and so the whole thing can be tuned (GridSearch over preprocessing params) and serialised as one artifact.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-b0aeebd", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "How would you encode cyclical features like hour of day?", "a": "<p>Map to the unit circle: sin(2π·hour/24) and cos(2π·hour/24), so 23:00 and 00:00 are close. Trees can also handle raw hour with splits, and one-hot works if each value has enough data.</p>"}, {"id": "data-prep-85971ed", "p": "data-prep.html", "g": "Data prep & feature engineering", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Which algorithms are sensitive to feature scaling and which aren't?", "a": "<p>Sensitive: kNN, K-means, SVM, PCA, LDA, regularised linear/logistic regression, anything trained by gradient descent (neural nets). Insensitive: decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting, Naive Bayes (per-feature distributions).</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-4a0a955", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Explain precision and recall with an example.", "a": "<p>Precision = TP/(TP+FP): of the transactions flagged as fraud, the fraction actually fraudulent. Recall = TP/(TP+FN): of all fraudulent transactions, the fraction we flagged. Raising the threshold increases precision and lowers recall; the right balance depends on the costs of false alarms vs misses.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-05740f3", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Why is accuracy a bad metric for imbalanced data?", "a": "<p>With 1% positives, predicting “negative” for everyone gives 99% accuracy while catching nothing. Accuracy is dominated by the majority class; use precision/recall, F1, PR-AUC, balanced accuracy or MCC, or a cost-based metric.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-de67908", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What does ROC-AUC mean intuitively?", "a": "<p>The probability that the model scores a randomly chosen positive higher than a randomly chosen negative. It summarises ranking quality across all thresholds; 0.5 is random, 1.0 perfect. It is insensitive to class balance and to monotonic transformations of scores.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-d43cd38", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "When would you prefer PR-AUC over ROC-AUC?", "a": "<p>When positives are rare and you care about performance on them (fraud, disease, defects). ROC-AUC uses FPR, whose denominator (all negatives) is huge, so many false positives barely move it; PR-AUC uses precision, which directly reflects false alarms relative to true detections.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-60f35bb", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the F1 score and when is it not appropriate?", "a": "<p>The harmonic mean of precision and recall, high only when both are high. Not appropriate when costs of FP and FN differ a lot (use Fβ or a cost function), when true negatives matter, or when you need threshold-free comparison (use AUCs).</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-134042e", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Macro vs micro vs weighted averaging?", "a": "<p>Macro: average per-class metrics equally — highlights minority-class performance. Weighted: average weighted by class support. Micro: aggregate TP/FP/FN across classes — dominated by large classes; equals accuracy for single-label multiclass.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-3b9df73", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is model calibration and how do you check it?", "a": "<p>A model is calibrated if predicted probabilities match observed frequencies (of cases predicted 0.8, ~80% are positive). Check with reliability diagrams, Expected Calibration Error, Brier score. Fix with Platt scaling or isotonic regression on held-out data. SVMs, Naive Bayes, boosted trees and class-weighted models often need it.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-ac6f32d", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "RMSE vs MAE — when to use which?", "a": "<p>RMSE squares errors, so it penalises large errors more and corresponds to predicting the mean; use it when big misses are especially costly. MAE treats all errors linearly, is robust to outliers and corresponds to the median. Report both: a big RMSE/MAE gap signals a few large errors.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-7550dd5", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Type I vs Type II error?", "a": "<p>Type I = false positive (rejecting a true null; flagging a legit transaction). Type II = false negative (failing to reject a false null; missing fraud). In hypothesis testing α controls Type I; power = 1 − β where β is the Type II rate.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-05daa5f", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "How do you choose a classification threshold?", "a": "<p>Not 0.5 by default. Options: maximise F1/Fβ on validation; minimise expected cost using a cost matrix (threshold = C_FP / (C_FP + C_FN) for calibrated probabilities); meet a constraint (precision ≥ 90%, or top-k capacity); Youden's J on ROC. Choose on validation data and confirm on test.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-6a5a421", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is log-loss and why might it be preferred to accuracy?", "a": "<p>The negative mean log-likelihood of true labels under predicted probabilities. It evaluates probability quality, heavily penalises confident wrong predictions, is a proper scoring rule (optimised by true probabilities), and is differentiable — accuracy ignores confidence entirely.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-7b54aa3", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Your model has 0.95 ROC-AUC but the business says it's useless. Why might that be?", "a": "<p>Extreme imbalance (precision at usable thresholds is tiny), evaluation on the wrong population/time period (leakage or drift), metric misaligned with the decision (they need top-k precision or calibrated risk), poor performance on the segment that matters, or predictions that aren't actionable/come too late.</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-c1e19b8", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is NDCG?", "a": "<p>Normalised Discounted Cumulative Gain: sums graded relevance of ranked items discounted by log₂(position + 1), divided by the ideal DCG so it lies in [0, 1]. It rewards putting highly relevant items at the top; used for search and recommendation ranking evaluation (NDCG@k).</p>"}, {"id": "metrics-c5f63ee", "p": "metrics.html", "g": "Evaluation metrics", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is MCC and why is it useful?", "a": "<p>Matthews Correlation Coefficient = (TP·TN − FP·FN)/√((TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN)), ranging −1 to 1. It uses all four confusion-matrix cells and is high only if the classifier does well on both classes — a reliable single metric under imbalance.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-b2e8f55", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is cross-validation and why use it?", "a": "<p>A resampling method that splits data into K folds, trains on K−1 and validates on the remaining one, rotating so every fold is validated once. It gives a lower-variance, more data-efficient estimate of generalisation than a single split, plus a spread (std) of performance, and is used for model selection and tuning.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-b4802b1", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What CV would you use for time-series data, and why?", "a": "<p>Time-ordered splits — expanding or sliding window (TimeSeriesSplit / rolling-origin backtests), optionally with a gap to prevent leakage via lag features and a horizon matching production. Random K-Fold would train on the future and validate on the past.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-4c42b95", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "When should you use GroupKFold?", "a": "<p>When multiple rows belong to the same entity (patient, user, device, store) and the model will be applied to new entities. Otherwise rows from the same entity appear in both train and validation, and the model gets credit for memorising entity-specific patterns.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-4a23df8", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is nested cross-validation?", "a": "<p>Two loops: inner CV selects hyperparameters on each outer training fold; the outer CV evaluates the tuned model on held-out outer folds. It gives an unbiased estimate of the performance of the whole model-building procedure, avoiding the optimism of reporting the best inner CV score.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-09fdaf1", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Grid search vs random search?", "a": "<p>Grid search evaluates every combination of predefined values — exhaustive but exponential in the number of hyperparameters and wasteful when only a few matter. Random search samples configurations from distributions; for the same budget it explores more distinct values of each important hyperparameter and usually finds equal or better results.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-5453161", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How does Bayesian optimisation work for hyperparameter tuning?", "a": "<p>It builds a probabilistic surrogate model of the objective (Gaussian process or TPE) from past trials and uses an acquisition function (expected improvement, UCB) to pick the next configuration balancing exploration and exploitation. It needs fewer evaluations than random search for expensive models; tools: Optuna, Hyperopt, scikit-optimize.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-933e7a0", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why is stratified K-Fold important for classification?", "a": "<p>It keeps class proportions consistent across folds, reducing variance of the estimate and preventing folds with very few (or zero) minority examples, which would make metrics like recall or AUC unstable or undefined.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-f0ab170", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the bias–variance trade-off in choosing K for K-Fold?", "a": "<p>Small K trains on less data, so estimates are pessimistically biased; large K (LOOCV) trains on nearly all data (low bias) but the training sets overlap heavily, so fold scores are highly correlated and the estimate can have high variance, plus more compute. K = 5–10 balances these.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-2b6deeb", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you avoid data leakage during cross-validation?", "a": "<p>Fit every data-dependent step (imputation, scaling, encoding, feature selection, resampling, target encoding) inside the CV loop via pipelines; respect group and time structure in splits; deduplicate before splitting; never look at test data while developing.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-ddd88ff", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is successive halving / Hyperband?", "a": "<p>Multi-fidelity tuning: evaluate many configurations with a small budget (few samples, epochs or trees), keep the top 1/η, multiply the budget by η, and repeat. Hyperband runs several such brackets with different trade-offs between number of configs and starting budget. sklearn: HalvingGridSearchCV / HalvingRandomSearchCV.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-db8d15a", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Difference between a validation curve and a learning curve?", "a": "<p>Validation curve: training and validation scores vs a hyperparameter value — find the complexity sweet spot. Learning curve: scores vs training-set size — diagnose whether more data (high variance) or more capacity (high bias) will help.</p>"}, {"id": "validation-fedf550", "p": "validation.html", "g": "Cross-validation & tuning", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "After cross-validation, which model do you deploy?", "a": "<p>Usually refit the chosen pipeline with the selected hyperparameters on all available training data (more data → better model); the CV score estimates its performance. Alternatives: an ensemble of the K fold models (common in competitions), or for time series, the model trained on the most recent window.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-d9c7a47", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Your model's performance is poor. How do you improve it?", "a": "<p>First validate the evaluation (right metric, production-like split, no leakage, baseline comparison). Then diagnose: high bias (train error high) → richer features/model, less regularisation; high variance (train ≪ validation error) → more data, regularisation, simpler model, bagging, early stopping. Do error analysis by slice and inspect worst cases; fix data/label issues; engineer domain features; tune hyperparameters; try ensembles; tune the threshold for the business metric. Validate gains online.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-5466a7b", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is stacking and how do you avoid leakage in it?", "a": "<p>Stacking trains a meta-model on the predictions of several base models. To avoid leakage the meta-model must be trained on out-of-fold predictions (each base model predicts on folds it wasn't trained on); at inference, base models trained on all data feed the meta-model. sklearn's StackingClassifier does this via its cv argument.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-ae110fb", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Bagging vs boosting vs stacking?", "a": "<p>Bagging: parallel training of the same model on bootstrap samples, averaged — reduces variance. Boosting: sequential models each fitting the ensemble's errors — reduces bias. Stacking: heterogeneous models combined by a learned meta-model — exploits complementary strengths.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-e9856dc", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What are SHAP values?", "a": "<p>Feature attributions based on Shapley values from cooperative game theory: each feature's average marginal contribution to the prediction over all feature orderings. They're additive (sum to prediction minus baseline), consistent, give local and (aggregated) global explanations, and are computed exactly and fast for trees with TreeSHAP.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-7f17567", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Permutation importance vs impurity importance vs SHAP?", "a": "<p>Impurity (MDI): fast, training-data based, biased toward high-cardinality features. Permutation: model-agnostic, measured on validation data as the score drop after shuffling a feature; can be misleading with correlated features. SHAP: per-prediction additive attributions with solid theory; global importance = mean |SHAP|; more expensive for non-tree models.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-1415a11", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is a partial dependence plot and its limitation?", "a": "<p>It shows the average model prediction as one (or two) features vary, marginalising over the others. Limitations: assumes features are independent (creates unrealistic combinations when correlated — ALE plots fix this) and averaging hides heterogeneous effects (ICE curves reveal them).</p>"}, {"id": "performance-56581bd", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you know if more data will help?", "a": "<p>Plot a learning curve: if the validation score is still rising and the train–validation gap is large at full size, more data should help (variance-limited). If both curves have plateaued close together, more data won't help — you need better features or a more expressive model.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-22fbeb1", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is error analysis and why does it matter?", "a": "<p>Systematically examining where the model fails: slicing metrics by segments, inspecting misclassified examples, categorising error types and estimating how much each category costs. It focuses effort on the fixes with the highest payoff (e.g., 40% of errors are from one mislabeled source) instead of blind tuning.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-19a2de8", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "LIME vs SHAP?", "a": "<p>LIME fits a local interpretable surrogate (sparse linear model) on perturbed samples around a prediction — model-agnostic but unstable and dependent on kernel width/sampling. SHAP has game-theoretic guarantees (local accuracy, consistency) and consistent global aggregation; KernelSHAP is like LIME with a specific weighting that recovers Shapley values.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-7467ea8", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How would you explain a complex model to a non-technical stakeholder?", "a": "<p>Lead with what it predicts and how well vs the current process (in business terms). Show the top drivers globally (SHAP summary in plain words), a couple of individual examples (“this customer is high risk mainly because of 3 missed payments and high utilisation”), the limitations/when not to trust it, and how it'll be monitored.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-eeb2efd", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "Model accuracy improved offline by 2% but the A/B test shows no lift. Why?", "a": "<p>Offline metric not aligned with the business metric; leakage or optimistic offline evaluation; train/serve skew; distribution shift between offline data and live traffic; feedback loops; the improvement is in segments that don't affect the outcome; latency increase hurting UX; underpowered experiment or novelty effects.</p>"}, {"id": "performance-9e1f269", "p": "performance.html", "g": "Improving models & explainability", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "When would you NOT use an ensemble?", "a": "<p>Strict latency/memory budgets, need for simple explanations (regulated credit decisions), marginal gains not worth maintenance and debugging complexity, or when a single well-tuned model is already near the noise ceiling.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-e870e88", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Explain a p-value to a non-technical person.", "a": "<p>“If the new design truly made no difference, how surprising would results like ours be?” A p-value of 0.01 means results this extreme would happen only 1% of the time by random chance alone — so we doubt the “no difference” assumption. It doesn't tell us how big or important the effect is.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-f38ec0e", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is the Central Limit Theorem and why does it matter?", "a": "<p>The distribution of the sample mean of i.i.d. variables with finite variance approaches a normal distribution as n grows, with standard error σ/√n, regardless of the original distribution. It justifies normal-based CIs and z/t-tests on averages of non-normal metrics in A/B tests.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-dcf88ce", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Type I vs Type II error — which is worse?", "a": "<p>Type I: false positive (ship a change that doesn't work), controlled by α. Type II: false negative (miss a real improvement), rate β; power = 1 − β. Which is worse depends on costs: in drug approval Type I is worse; in early-stage product exploration a Type II error (killing a good idea) may cost more.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-49ac996", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you determine the sample size for an A/B test?", "a": "<p>Specify baseline metric value and variance, the minimum detectable effect worth acting on, α (0.05) and power (0.8); use the power formula (n ≈ 16σ²/δ² per group for α=0.05, power 0.8) or a calculator. Convert to duration with traffic, rounding up to full weeks.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-f2857e2", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is statistical power and how can you increase it?", "a": "<p>The probability of detecting a true effect of a given size. Increase it with more samples, larger effects (bolder changes), lower variance (CUPED/covariate adjustment, better metrics, stratification, trimming outliers), higher α, or one-sided tests when justified.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-356b3ab", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why is peeking at A/B test results a problem?", "a": "<p>Each look is another chance to cross the significance threshold by luck, so stopping as soon as p &lt; 0.05 inflates the false-positive rate far above 5%. Pre-commit to a sample size, or use sequential methods (group sequential designs with alpha spending, always-valid p-values).</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-5661eed", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you handle multiple testing?", "a": "<p>Pre-specify one primary metric; correct for many comparisons with Bonferroni (α/m, conservative, controls family-wise error) or Benjamini–Hochberg (controls false discovery rate, more power). Treat segment findings as hypotheses for follow-up tests.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-dc459bc", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is sample ratio mismatch?", "a": "<p>When the observed split of users between variants differs significantly from the designed split (e.g. 50.8/49.2 with millions of users, χ² p &lt; 0.001). It signals bugs in randomisation, assignment, redirects or logging, and invalidates the results until fixed.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-eb85d00", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What would you do if your A/B test is significant for one metric but a guardrail metric got worse?", "a": "<p>Quantify both effects with CIs and translate to business value; check whether the guardrail drop is real (SRM, novelty, segments); discuss the trade-off with stakeholders; consider iterating on the design to keep the gain without the harm; don't ship blindly on the primary metric.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-27575e3", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is Simpson's paradox?", "a": "<p>A trend in aggregated data reverses within every subgroup because of a confounding variable that changes the mix (e.g. treatment looks worse overall but better within each severity level because sicker patients got it more often). Look at stratified results and think causally.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-2754ef0", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "t-test vs z-test vs chi-square test?", "a": "<p>z-test: means/proportions with known variance or large samples. t-test: means with estimated variance (small/moderate n); Welch's version for unequal variances. χ² test: categorical counts — goodness of fit or independence (e.g. conversion vs variant), equivalent to the two-proportion z-test for 2×2 tables.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-2b8e72c", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is CUPED?", "a": "<p>Controlled-experiment Using Pre-Experiment Data: adjust the metric with a pre-period covariate, Y' = Y − θ(X − mean X), θ = Cov(X, Y)/Var(X). It keeps the estimate unbiased (randomisation makes X balanced) while reducing variance by a factor (1 − ρ²), shortening experiments.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-f74f78e", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is a confidence interval? Interpret a 95% CI of [1.2%, 3.5%] lift.", "a": "<p>The procedure produces intervals that contain the true lift 95% of the time across repeated experiments. Here: the data are consistent with a true lift between 1.2% and 3.5%; since 0 is excluded the effect is significant at α=0.05, and even the low end may or may not clear the practical-significance bar.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-41d48fc", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "A test with 99% sensitivity and 99% specificity for a disease with 0.1% prevalence — P(disease | positive)?", "a": "<p>Per 100,000 people: 100 sick → 99 true positives; 99,900 healthy → 999 false positives. P = 99 / (99 + 999) ≈ 9%. The base rate dominates — the base-rate fallacy.</p>"}, {"id": "statistics-a40962e", "p": "statistics.html", "g": "Statistics & A/B testing", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "When can't you run an A/B test, and what then?", "a": "<p>Ethical/legal constraints, network effects you can't isolate, one-off launches, too little traffic, or long-term outcomes. Use quasi-experiments: difference-in-differences, synthetic control, regression discontinuity, instrumental variables, propensity score matching, interrupted time series — with careful assumption checks.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-b349401", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is variance, and why do we square the deviations?", "a": "<p>Variance is the average squared distance of values from their mean — a measure of spread. Squaring makes all deviations positive (otherwise they sum to zero), penalises large deviations more, and gives convenient mathematics: variances of independent variables add, and squared error is differentiable. The cost is that variance is in squared units, so we usually report the standard deviation (its square root) for interpretation. Absolute deviations (MAD) are a robust alternative.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-8d79ca6", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Why does the sample variance divide by n − 1?", "a": "<p>Deviations are measured from the sample mean, which is computed from the same data and sits closer to the data than the true mean does, so the sum of squared deviations is systematically too small. Only n − 1 deviations are free (they must sum to zero). Dividing by n − 1 corrects the bias exactly: E[s²] = σ². It matters for small n; for large n the difference is negligible. (The square root, s, is still slightly biased for σ.)</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-09a1783", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Standard deviation vs standard error?", "a": "<p>Standard deviation describes how spread out individual observations are. Standard error describes how uncertain an estimate is — for the mean, SE = SD/√n. More data doesn't change the SD of the population but shrinks the SE. Confidence intervals and tests use SE; descriptions of the data use SD.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-bc653f4", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is covariance and what is its weakness?", "a": "<p>The average product of the deviations of two variables from their means: positive when they tend to be above/below their means together, negative when they move oppositely, near zero with no linear relationship. Weakness: its magnitude depends on the units (multiply one variable by 1,000 and covariance grows 1,000-fold), so it can't tell you how strong the relationship is or compare pairs of variables. Only its sign is easy to read.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-50d2d3f", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "What is the difference between covariance and correlation?", "a": "<p>Correlation is covariance standardised by both standard deviations: r = Cov(X, Y)/(s_X s_Y). That makes it unit-free and bounded in [−1, 1], so it measures the strength (not just the direction) of a linear relationship and can be compared across variable pairs. Rescaling or shifting either variable leaves correlation unchanged; rescaling changes covariance. Covariance is still needed in its own right — e.g. the covariance matrix in PCA, portfolio variance, multivariate Gaussians.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-52f5f72", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How is correlation different from regression?", "a": "<p>Correlation is a symmetric, unit-free summary of how tightly two variables move together linearly — r(X, Y) = r(Y, X), no prediction, no direction. Regression fits a directional equation to predict Y from X: the slope has units (Y per unit X), regressing Y on X differs from X on Y, it gives predictions and residuals, and with several predictors it estimates each effect holding the others fixed, with confidence intervals. They're linked: slope = r·s_y/s_x, and in simple regression R² = r². So with standardised variables the slope equals r.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-7a974a3", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is cosine similarity and how is it different from Pearson correlation?", "a": "<p>Cosine similarity is the cosine of the angle between two vectors: A·B/(‖A‖‖B‖). It ignores length and measures only direction, from −1 to 1. Pearson correlation is exactly the cosine similarity of the two vectors after subtracting each one's mean. So the difference is centring: cosine is affected by adding a constant to a vector, correlation isn't. Practically, cosine compares rows (documents, embeddings, users) where magnitude means “how much” rather than “what kind”; correlation compares variables.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-893058a", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How does cosine similarity relate to regression?", "a": "<p>Both are built from dot products. Regression through the origin (no intercept) has slope x·y/x·x = cosθ·‖y‖/‖x‖ — cosine times a ratio of lengths. Ordinary regression with an intercept centres the data first, turning cosine into Pearson r, and its slope is r·s_y/s_x. But the purposes differ: cosine is a symmetric similarity score between two vectors with no units; regression is an asymmetric prediction model with units, residuals and inference. And note that cosine usually compares two observations across features, while regression relates columns across observations.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-ad1cdc9", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "When would you use cosine similarity instead of Euclidean distance?", "a": "<p>When direction matters but magnitude doesn't: text (document length shouldn't matter, only the mix of words), embeddings, user preference profiles, high-dimensional sparse vectors where Euclidean distances are dominated by vector length and concentrate. Use Euclidean when absolute size matters (physical measurements, scaled numeric features in K-means). For L2-normalised vectors the two give identical rankings since ‖A − B‖² = 2 − 2cosθ.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-9fc45af", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Does zero correlation mean the variables are independent?", "a": "<p>No. Pearson correlation measures only linear association. y = x² with x symmetric around 0 has correlation ≈ 0 even though y is a function of x. Independence implies zero correlation, not the reverse — except when the variables are jointly normal. To detect non-linear dependence use scatter plots, Spearman (for monotonic), mutual information, distance correlation or a flexible model.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-80c80a6", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Pearson vs Spearman vs Kendall — when do you use each?", "a": "<p>Pearson: linear relationships between roughly continuous, outlier-free variables. Spearman: Pearson on ranks — any monotonic relationship, robust to outliers, ordinal data. Kendall's τ: based on concordant/discordant pairs — better for small samples and many ties, with a direct probability interpretation. If Pearson and Spearman disagree a lot, look for outliers or curvature.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-d253f05", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "A correlation of 0.3 is statistically significant. Is it a strong relationship?", "a": "<p>Significance only says it's unlikely to be exactly zero; with a large sample even tiny correlations are significant. Strength: r = 0.3 means r² = 0.09 — a straight line explains 9% of the variance. Whether that's useful depends on the context (in noisy human behaviour it can be valuable; for prediction of an individual it's weak). Report the effect size and confidence interval, not just the p-value.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-dee385f", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Give ways a correlation can mislead you.", "a": "<p>Non-linear relationships (r ≈ 0 despite dependence); outliers creating or masking correlation; confounders (both driven by a third variable); Simpson's paradox (reverses within subgroups); restricted range (only hired candidates); spurious correlation between trending time series; ecological fallacy (group-level correlation applied to individuals); reverse causation; and large samples making trivial correlations “significant”. Always plot the data — Anscombe's quartet shows four datasets with identical r and very different shapes.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-b3e1cc0", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is a covariance matrix and where is it used in ML?", "a": "<p>A d × d matrix with variances on the diagonal and pairwise covariances off the diagonal; symmetric and positive semi-definite. Uses: PCA (its eigenvectors are the principal components); multivariate normal distributions, GMMs, LDA/QDA; Mahalanobis distance for outliers; portfolio risk; detecting multicollinearity; Kalman filters. Estimating it is hard in high dimensions (d² parameters), so shrinkage estimators (Ledoit–Wolf) are used.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-e55363b", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is Var(X + Y) and Var(X − Y)? Why does it matter?", "a": "<p>Var(X ± Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y) ± 2Cov(X, Y). For independent variables the covariance term vanishes and variances add (even for a difference). Applications: an A/B test difference has variance σ²_A/n_A + σ²_B/n_B; paired designs reduce variance because the measurements are positively correlated; portfolios of negatively correlated assets have lower risk; averaging correlated models in an ensemble reduces variance less than averaging independent ones.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-3762fa6", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is mutual information and how does it compare with correlation?", "a": "<p>Mutual information measures how much knowing one variable reduces uncertainty about the other: I(X; Y) = KL(p(x, y) ‖ p(x)p(y)). It's zero only if the variables are independent and it captures any kind of dependence, linear or not, numeric or categorical. Downsides: it has no sign, its scale isn't bounded like r, and it's harder to estimate from data (needs binning or kNN estimators). Used in feature selection and decision-tree information gain.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-9dfc88e", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "How is simulation different from machine learning?", "a": "<p>Simulation goes from a model of how a system works to generated outcomes: you specify rules and parameter distributions and run them many times to see what happens, including in scenarios never observed. Machine learning goes from observed data to a learned model that predicts outcomes for new cases like those in the data. Simulation needs mechanism knowledge and is validated by calibration and sensitivity analysis; ML needs representative data and is validated on held-out data. Simulation is good for “what if we change the policy?”; ML is good for “what will happen for this customer?”. They're often combined: ML estimates simulation inputs, simulations generate training data, and ML surrogates replace slow simulators.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-a050416", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is Monte Carlo simulation and how accurate is it?", "a": "<p>Estimating a quantity (an expectation, probability, integral or distribution) by drawing many random samples from a known process and averaging the results. Its standard error is σ/√n — independent of dimension, but slow: 100 times more samples for one more decimal digit. Report the estimate with its Monte Carlo error. Improvements: variance reduction (antithetic variates, control variates, importance sampling, common random numbers when comparing options), quasi-random sequences.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-669d294", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "When would you choose simulation over a predictive model?", "a": "<p>When there's little or no historical data for the decision (a new warehouse layout, a pricing policy never tried); when you need to evaluate interventions and counterfactuals, not just predict; when the mechanism is well understood (queues, physics, inventory rules); when rare extreme events matter (risk, stress testing); or to quantify uncertainty propagation. Choose ML when you have plenty of representative data and the question is prediction within familiar conditions.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-c5eef2d", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is a surrogate model and the sim-to-real gap?", "a": "<p>A surrogate model is an ML model trained on input–output pairs from an expensive simulator, so it can approximate the simulator in milliseconds — used for optimisation, design exploration and real-time control. The sim-to-real gap is the drop in performance when a model trained on simulated data meets real data, because the simulator omits real-world noise, physics or behaviour. Mitigations: domain randomisation, calibrating the simulator against real data, and fine-tuning on a small real dataset.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-9bca39c", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you validate a simulation model?", "a": "<p>Verification: does the code do what the model specifies (unit tests, extreme-value tests, comparison with analytical results for simple cases)? Validation: does the model match reality — reproduce historical outcomes, expert face validity, compare output distributions with observed data. Sensitivity analysis: which assumptions drive the results; uncertainty analysis on inputs. Run enough replications to make Monte Carlo error small and report it.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-fb8d158", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Bootstrap, permutation test and Monte Carlo — how are they related?", "a": "<p>All three use repeated random sampling. Monte Carlo samples from a <i>known</i> model. The bootstrap samples with replacement from the <i>observed data</i>, treating it as the population, to estimate the sampling distribution of a statistic (standard errors, CIs). A permutation test shuffles labels to build the distribution of a statistic under the null hypothesis of no effect, giving a p-value without distributional assumptions.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-00557f7", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the difference between statistical modelling and machine learning?", "a": "<p>Mostly the goal. Statistical modelling emphasises inference — interpretable parameters, uncertainty, hypothesis tests, explicit assumptions, often with small data. Machine learning emphasises predictive accuracy on new data, judged by validation, with flexible models and fewer assumptions, often with large data. The tools overlap (logistic regression is both) and the line is blurry, but the goal drives the choices of model, validation and what you report.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-8c0d635", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Mean vs median — when do you report each?", "a": "<p>The mean uses every value and suits symmetric data and totals (revenue = mean × users); it's pulled by outliers and skew. The median is robust and describes the “typical” value in skewed data such as income, house prices or session time. In a right-skewed distribution mean &gt; median. Report both when they differ much, and choose by the question: total budget → mean; typical customer → median.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-20c0f73", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is regression to the mean? Give an example where it misleads.", "a": "<p>Extreme observations contain luck, which doesn't repeat, so re-measuring extreme cases yields values closer to the average. Example: the 50 worst-performing stores get a new training programme and improve next quarter — but they would have improved partly anyway. Students who score highest on one test score lower on the next. Fix: compare with a randomised control group selected the same way. Expected next value ≈ mean + reliability × (current − mean).</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-04b8e8f", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Explain maximum likelihood estimation with a simple example.", "a": "<p>Choose the parameter values that make the observed data most probable. Coin flipped 10 times, 7 heads: likelihood L(p) = p⁷(1 − p)³; maximise log L = 7 log p + 3 log(1 − p); derivative 7/p − 3/(1 − p) = 0 → p̂ = 0.7. MLE gives the sample mean for a normal mean, and minimising squared error in regression is MLE under Gaussian noise; log-loss is MLE for Bernoulli outcomes. MLEs are consistent and efficient for large samples but can overfit small ones — adding a prior gives MAP.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-f43f6fd", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Frequentist vs Bayesian statistics — what's the difference?", "a": "<p>Frequentists treat parameters as fixed unknowns and probability as long-run frequency; they report point estimates, confidence intervals and p-values about the procedure. Bayesians treat parameters as random with a prior distribution, update it with the likelihood to get a posterior, and report credible intervals (“95% probability the effect lies here”) and probabilities of hypotheses. Bayesian methods incorporate prior knowledge and handle small samples and sequential decisions naturally, at the cost of choosing priors and heavier computation.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-bfdcb0b", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "Two children, at least one is a boy. What is the probability both are boys?", "a": "<p>Equally likely families: BB, BG, GB, GG. “At least one boy” rules out GG, leaving three, of which one is BB → 1/3. (If you're told the <i>elder</i> is a boy, the answer is 1/2 — the information changes the conditioning set.) The point interviewers want: define the sample space and condition on exactly what you know.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-aeddb77", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 1, "q": "On average, how many rolls of a die until you see a 6? How many coin flips until two heads in a row?", "a": "<p>Rolls to a 6: geometric with p = 1/6, mean 1/p = 6. Two heads in a row: let E be the expected flips from the start. Condition on the first flips: T (prob ½) wastes one flip → back to start; HT (prob ¼) wastes two; HH (prob ¼) finishes in two. E = ½(1 + E) + ¼(2 + E) + ¼·2 → E = 6. Setting up recursive expectations is the reusable trick.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-c624b2c", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Calls arrive at 3 per hour. What is the probability of no calls in 20 minutes?", "a": "<p>Poisson process: in 20 minutes the expected count is λ = 3 × 1/3 = 1. P(0) = e^{−1} ≈ 0.368. Equivalently, inter-arrival times are exponential with rate 3/hour: P(wait &gt; 1/3 hour) = e^{−3 × 1/3}. The exponential is memoryless — having waited 10 minutes doesn't change the remaining wait distribution.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-32c7fc5", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What's the difference between independent and mutually exclusive events?", "a": "<p>Independent: knowing one occurred doesn't change the probability of the other, P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B). Mutually exclusive: they can't both happen, P(A ∩ B) = 0. Two mutually exclusive events with non-zero probabilities are therefore strongly <i>dependent</i>: if A happened, B certainly didn't.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-0eab5b3", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the birthday problem and why do data scientists care?", "a": "<p>With 23 people, the probability that at least two share a birthday is about 50.7%, because the number of pairs (253) grows quadratically. Computed via the complement: 1 − ∏(365 − i)/365. Relevance: hash collisions and ID collisions, how quickly duplicates appear when sampling, and the general lesson that “some coincidence among many comparisons” is likely — the same reason multiple testing produces false positives.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-f1f4235", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the difference between a confounder, a mediator and a collider?", "a": "<p>Confounder: causes both treatment and outcome (age affects both exercise and health) — adjust for it to remove bias. Mediator: lies on the causal path from treatment to outcome (exercise → weight → health) — adjusting for it removes part of the effect you want to measure. Collider: caused by both treatment and outcome — adjusting for or selecting on it creates a spurious association (Berkson's paradox: among hospital patients, two unrelated diseases look negatively correlated). Draw the causal graph before choosing controls.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-41e5fdb", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is survivorship bias? Give an ML example.", "a": "<p>Drawing conclusions only from cases that “survived” a selection process. Classic: armour was proposed where returning planes had bullet holes — but planes hit elsewhere never returned. ML examples: training a churn model only on customers still active at a later date; a credit model trained only on approved loans; a stock-picking model trained on companies still listed today; evaluating a recommender only on items that were shown. Fix: reconstruct the full population at the decision point (point-in-time data).</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-2b32356", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Statistical significance vs practical significance?", "a": "<p>Statistical significance says an effect is unlikely to be exactly zero given the noise; with enough data, tiny effects become significant. Practical significance asks whether the effect is big enough to matter for the decision (cost, revenue, user experience). Report effect sizes with confidence intervals (e.g. +0.2 pp conversion, CI 0.05–0.35 pp, worth ₹X/month) and compare with a minimum effect of interest decided in advance.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-80ea4ee", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Parametric vs non-parametric tests — when do you use non-parametric ones?", "a": "<p>Parametric tests (t-test, ANOVA) assume a distribution (roughly normal means, equal variances) and are more powerful when assumptions hold. Non-parametric tests (Mann–Whitney U, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Kruskal–Wallis, permutation tests) make fewer assumptions and work on ranks — use them for small samples from clearly non-normal data, ordinal data, or heavy outliers. With large samples, the CLT often makes t-tests on means fine even for skewed data.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-bfe6efd", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "How do you check whether data are normally distributed, and does it matter?", "a": "<p>Visual checks first: histogram and Q-Q plot (points on the line if normal). Formal tests (Shapiro–Wilk, Kolmogorov–Smirnov, Anderson–Darling) reject trivial deviations in large samples and lack power in small ones. Whether it matters depends on use: for linear regression it's the residuals, not features, and only for small-sample inference; t-tests on means rely on the CLT; many ML models assume nothing about normality.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-98a9d1e", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is a heavy-tailed distribution and why should you care?", "a": "<p>One where extreme values are far more likely than under a normal distribution (power laws, log-normal-like revenue, claim sizes, wealth). Consequences: the sample mean converges slowly and is dominated by a few observations; the variance may be huge or infinite; A/B tests on revenue need many more users; squared-error models chase outliers. Responses: use medians/quantiles, log transforms, winsorising, robust losses, bootstrap CIs, and analyse the tail separately.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-a0083cc", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "What is the bias–variance decomposition of an estimator's error?", "a": "<p>MSE(θ̂) = Bias(θ̂)² + Var(θ̂). An unbiased estimator isn't automatically best: a slightly biased one with much lower variance can have smaller MSE — the logic behind shrinkage (Ridge regression, James–Stein). An estimator is consistent if it converges to the true value as n grows. The same decomposition, applied to predictions, gives the bias–variance trade-off of ML models.</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-a9b3fa2", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 3, "q": "What is KL divergence, and how is it related to cross-entropy?", "a": "<p>KL(P‖Q) = Σ p(x) log(p(x)/q(x)) measures how much extra information is needed when using Q to approximate P; it's ≥ 0, zero only when P = Q, and not symmetric. Cross-entropy H(P, Q) = H(P) + KL(P‖Q); since the true label distribution's entropy H(P) is fixed, minimising log-loss (cross-entropy) is the same as minimising KL from the data to the model. Also used in t-SNE, VAEs, drift detection (alongside PSI and Jensen–Shannon).</p>"}, {"id": "core-stats-88398a5", "p": "core-stats.html", "g": "Variance, correlation, similarity & simulation", "grp": "Foundations", "l": 2, "q": "Stratified vs cluster vs systematic sampling — when do you use each?", "a": "<p>Stratified: split the population into groups (region, class) and sample within each — guarantees representation and reduces variance; the idea behind stratified train/test splits. Cluster: randomly pick whole groups (stores, schools) and survey everyone in them — cheaper logistics but higher variance because members of a cluster are similar. Systematic: every k-th unit from a list — simple, but dangerous if the list has a periodic pattern.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-5a4ed26", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What are the assumptions of linear regression?", "a": "<p>Linearity in parameters; independence of errors (no autocorrelation); homoscedasticity (constant error variance); normality of errors (for inference); no perfect multicollinearity; exogeneity (errors uncorrelated with features / no omitted confounders). Mention which ones affect <i>predictions</i> (linearity, exogeneity for causal reading) vs <i>inference</i> (independence, homoscedasticity, normality).</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-a35a49d", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is homoscedasticity and how do you detect and fix heteroscedasticity?", "a": "<p>Homoscedasticity = residual variance is the same at every level of the predictors. Heteroscedasticity shows up as a funnel in residuals-vs-fitted. Detect with that plot, the scale-location plot, or Breusch–Pagan / White tests. Consequences: coefficients remain unbiased, but OLS is no longer efficient and standard errors, t-stats, p-values and CIs are wrong. Fixes: transform y (log, Box-Cox, sqrt), Weighted Least Squares with weights ∝ 1/variance, or heteroscedasticity-consistent (HC3 / White) standard errors.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-f1b69ed", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is multicollinearity? How do you detect and handle it?", "a": "<p>High correlation among predictors. It inflates the variance of coefficient estimates (unstable, sign flips, large SEs, insignificant individually but jointly significant) but does <b>not</b> hurt predictive accuracy much. Detect: correlation matrix, VIF (&gt;5–10), condition number of X. Handle: drop one of the correlated features, combine them (ratio, average, PCA), collect more data, or use Ridge which stabilises coefficients.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-13f9050", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is VIF and how is it computed?", "a": "<p>Variance Inflation Factor for feature j: regress \\(x_j\\) on all other features, take that \\(R_j^2\\), then \\(\\text{VIF}_j = 1/(1-R_j^2)\\). It is the factor by which the variance of \\(\\hat\\beta_j\\) is inflated relative to uncorrelated features. VIF = 1 none, 5 → \\(R^2_j\\)=0.8, 10 → 0.9.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-2350764", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Derive the OLS closed-form solution.", "a": "<p>Minimise \\(\\lVert y-X\\beta\\rVert^2 = y^Ty - 2\\beta^TX^Ty + \\beta^TX^TX\\beta\\). Gradient: \\(-2X^Ty + 2X^TX\\beta = 0\\) ⇒ \\(X^TX\\beta=X^Ty\\) ⇒ \\(\\hat\\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^Ty\\), valid when \\(X^TX\\) is invertible (full column rank). The Hessian \\(2X^TX\\) is positive semi-definite, so it is a global minimum.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-1e65f4a", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you use gradient descent instead of the normal equation?", "a": "<p>When p is large (inverting a p×p matrix is O(p³)), when n is huge or data doesn't fit in memory (mini-batch/SGD streams), for online learning, or when adding non-closed-form penalties (Lasso uses coordinate descent). Normal equation: no learning rate, no scaling needed, exact — fine for small/medium p.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-f256fd6", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why do we minimise squared error rather than absolute error?", "a": "<p>Squared error is differentiable everywhere and convex with a closed-form solution; it equals the MLE under Gaussian noise; it estimates the conditional mean; and it penalises large errors more. Absolute error (MAE/LAD) estimates the conditional median, is robust to outliers, corresponds to Laplace noise, but has no closed form (solved via linear programming) and a non-smooth gradient at 0.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-3e067e6", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Difference between R² and adjusted R²?", "a": "<p>R² = 1 − RSS/TSS, the share of variance explained; it never decreases when features are added (on training data). Adjusted R² = 1 − (1 − R²)(n − 1)/(n − p − 1) penalises the number of predictors, increasing only if a new feature improves the fit more than expected by chance. Use adjusted R² (or better, CV error / AIC / BIC) to compare models with different numbers of features.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-4caba6b", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Can R² be negative?", "a": "<p>On training data with an intercept, no (OLS is at least as good as the mean). On test data, or without an intercept, yes: it means the model predicts worse than a constant equal to the mean of y.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-aa5a7ec", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you interpret coefficients when y is log-transformed?", "a": "<p>log(y) = β₀ + β₁x: a one-unit increase in x multiplies y by \\(e^{\\beta_1}\\), ≈ a 100·β₁ % change for small β₁. log(y) ~ log(x): β₁ is an elasticity (1 % change in x → β₁ % change in y). y ~ log(x): a 1 % increase in x changes y by β₁/100 units.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-1f8faea", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What happens if you duplicate a feature (or duplicate all rows)?", "a": "<p>Duplicate feature: perfect collinearity, \\(X^TX\\) singular; OLS has infinitely many solutions (the pseudo-inverse / sklearn splits the weight). Ridge splits it evenly. Duplicating all rows: coefficients unchanged, but standard errors shrink by √2 — falsely inflated confidence, a classic data-leakage-in-inference trap.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-06deb78", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are outliers, leverage points and influential points?", "a": "<p>Outlier: unusual y given x (large residual). High leverage: unusual x (far from the feature mean; diagonal of the hat matrix \\(H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T\\)). Influential: removing it materially changes the fit — usually high leverage <i>and</i> large residual; measured by Cook's distance. OLS is sensitive because errors are squared.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-7219bbe", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What does the p-value of a coefficient mean?", "a": "<p>The probability of observing a t-statistic at least as extreme as ours if the true coefficient were 0 (and assumptions hold). Small p → evidence that the feature has a non-zero association with y given the other features. It is not the probability that the coefficient is zero, and not a measure of effect size or causality.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-2a8ba75", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is omitted-variable bias?", "a": "<p>If a variable z affects y and is correlated with an included x, leaving z out makes x's coefficient absorb z's effect: \\(E[\\hat\\beta_x] = \\beta_x + \\beta_z\\cdot\\delta\\), where δ is the slope from regressing z on x. It violates exogeneity, so coefficients are biased — no amount of data fixes it. Example: ice-cream sales vs drowning, omitted temperature.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-ae13137", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does linear regression handle categorical variables?", "a": "<p>One-hot/dummy encode with k − 1 columns (drop a baseline) to avoid the dummy-variable trap when an intercept exists; each coefficient is the difference vs the baseline. For high cardinality consider target encoding (with CV to avoid leakage) or regularisation.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-b4b5212", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Do you need to scale features for linear regression?", "a": "<p>Not for OLS predictions — the closed-form solution is scale-equivariant (coefficients just rescale). You <b>do</b> need scaling for gradient descent (faster, stable convergence), for regularised models (penalty treats all coefficients equally), and to compare coefficient magnitudes.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-bdc2829", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What if p &gt; n?", "a": "<p>\\(X^TX\\) is rank-deficient: infinitely many zero-training-error solutions, perfect overfit. Use regularisation (Ridge always has a unique solution, Lasso selects at most n features), dimensionality reduction (PCA regression, PLS), or feature selection.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-1dbca18", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Explain the bias–variance of OLS and why Ridge can beat it.", "a": "<p>OLS is unbiased (under assumptions) but may have high variance with many / correlated features. Ridge introduces a small bias by shrinking coefficients, which can reduce variance by more, lowering expected test MSE. Gauss–Markov only says OLS is best among <i>unbiased</i> linear estimators.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-929ec04", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How would you check if a linear model is appropriate for your data?", "a": "<p>Scatter / pair plots, residuals vs fitted and vs each feature (no pattern), Q-Q plot, compare CV error with a flexible model (e.g., gradient boosting): if the flexible model is much better, important non-linearity/interactions are missing.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-7ed04f7", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the hat matrix and what are its properties?", "a": "<p>\\(H = X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T\\), it “puts the hat on y”: \\(\\hat y = Hy\\). It is symmetric and idempotent (a projection), with trace = p + 1 (number of parameters). Diagonal elements \\(h_{ii}\\) are leverages; average leverage is (p+1)/n and points with \\(h_{ii} &gt; 2(p+1)/n\\) are high-leverage.</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-d18ce42", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Correlation vs regression coefficient?", "a": "<p>Simple regression slope = \\(r\\cdot s_y/s_x\\). Correlation is symmetric, unit-free, in [−1, 1]; the slope is directional (y on x ≠ x on y) and in units of y per unit of x. In simple regression, R² = r².</p>"}, {"id": "linear-regression-0155188", "p": "linear-regression.html", "g": "Linear regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the difference between a confidence interval and a prediction interval?", "a": "<p>A confidence interval covers the <i>mean</i> response \\(E[y\\mid x]\\) — uncertainty only in the estimated line. A prediction interval covers a <i>new individual</i> observation — adds the irreducible noise σ², so it is always wider and does not shrink to zero with more data.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-0d34a64", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is the difference between L1 and L2 regularisation?", "a": "<p>L1 (Lasso) penalises the sum of absolute coefficients, producing sparse models (exact zeros → feature selection), is non-differentiable at 0, and picks one of a group of correlated features. L2 (Ridge) penalises the sum of squares, shrinks all coefficients smoothly without zeroing, has a closed-form solution, and handles multicollinearity by spreading weight. Bayesian: L1 ↔ Laplace prior, L2 ↔ Gaussian prior.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-948b94e", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why does L1 produce sparse solutions but L2 doesn't?", "a": "<p>Geometrically the L1 constraint region is a diamond (cross-polytope) whose corners lie on the axes; the elliptical RSS contours typically first touch it at a corner where some coordinates are 0. The L2 ball is smooth, so contact is generically off-axis. Analytically, the L1 subgradient is a constant ±α that can overpower a small data gradient and hold the coefficient at 0 (soft-thresholding), whereas the L2 gradient 2αβ vanishes near 0.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-cd62474", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why must you scale features before Ridge/Lasso?", "a": "<p>The penalty is applied equally to all coefficients, but a coefficient's size depends on the feature's units. Unscaled, features with large ranges get small coefficients and are barely penalised, while small-range features are penalised heavily — the regularisation becomes arbitrary. Standardise inside a pipeline so the scaler is fit only on training folds.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-21fc862", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does Ridge help with multicollinearity?", "a": "<p>With correlated features \\(X^TX\\) has tiny eigenvalues, so \\((X^TX)^{-1}\\) blows up the variance of β̂. Ridge adds α to every eigenvalue: \\((X^TX+\\alpha I)\\) is always invertible and well-conditioned, which stabilises the coefficients (at the cost of some bias) and splits weight among correlated features.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-dfd76b4", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you choose Elastic Net over Lasso?", "a": "<p>When features are strongly correlated (Lasso arbitrarily keeps one and is unstable; Elastic Net's L2 part gives a grouping effect), and when p ≫ n (Lasso can select at most n features). It keeps Lasso's sparsity while gaining Ridge's stability.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-8494234", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose α?", "a": "<p>Cross-validation over a log-spaced grid (RidgeCV, LassoCV, ElasticNetCV or GridSearchCV with the scaler inside a Pipeline). Pick the α with the lowest CV error or, for a simpler model, the largest α within one standard error of the minimum. Information criteria (AIC/BIC via LassoLarsIC) are faster alternatives.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-07a2870", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What happens as α → 0 and α → ∞?", "a": "<p>α → 0: the solution approaches OLS (high variance if overfitting). α → ∞: all coefficients go to 0 and the model predicts the mean (intercept only) — maximum bias. In between lies the bias–variance sweet spot.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-4b51135", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Is the intercept regularised? Why not?", "a": "<p>No. Penalising it would make predictions depend on the arbitrary origin of y (shifting y by a constant would change the fit). sklearn centres the data and fits the intercept separately.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-a1af7f9", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Explain regularisation from a Bayesian perspective.", "a": "<p>The posterior ∝ likelihood × prior. With Gaussian noise, the negative log-posterior is RSS/(2σ²) − log p(β). A Gaussian prior \\(\\beta_j\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\tau^2)\\) gives the penalty \\(\\sum\\beta_j^2/(2\\tau^2)\\) → Ridge with α = σ²/τ². A Laplace prior gives an L1 penalty → Lasso. So the MAP estimate equals the regularised solution; a tighter prior (small τ) = larger α.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-5387227", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Lasso selected different features on two bootstrap samples. Why, and what would you do?", "a": "<p>Lasso is unstable under correlation: among correlated features it picks one nearly at random, and small data changes flip the choice. Remedies: Elastic Net, stability selection (fraction of bootstrap fits in which each feature is selected), group Lasso for known groups, or clustering correlated features first. And don't over-interpret Lasso's selection as causal importance.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-2d62620", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Does regularisation reduce bias or variance?", "a": "<p>It increases bias and reduces variance. It helps when the variance reduction outweighs the added bias — typically with many features, small n, noisy data or collinearity.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-d09b776", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the relation between C in LogisticRegression and α?", "a": "<p>C is the inverse of regularisation strength (C ≈ 1/α, scaled by n depending on the formulation). Large C = weak regularisation; small C = strong regularisation. Same idea in SVMs.</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-7eaf74f", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why can't Lasso be solved with the normal equation or plain gradient descent?", "a": "<p>|β| isn't differentiable at 0, so there's no closed form and the gradient is undefined at exactly the points we care about. We use coordinate descent with soft-thresholding (sklearn), LARS (which traces the whole path), proximal gradient (ISTA/FISTA), or subgradient methods (which don't produce exact zeros).</p>"}, {"id": "regularization-43a7121", "p": "regularization.html", "g": "Ridge, Lasso & Elastic Net", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Name other forms of regularisation in ML.", "a": "<p>Early stopping, dropout, weight decay, data augmentation, tree constraints (max_depth, min_samples_leaf, pruning), shrinkage/learning rate and subsampling in boosting, limiting the number of features (feature selection), ensembling, label smoothing, adding noise.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-b6220d3", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why is it called “regression” if it's used for classification?", "a": "<p>It regresses the log-odds of the positive class on the features (a generalised linear model with a logit link). It outputs a continuous probability; classification comes from thresholding that probability.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-d5cbbdb", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why do we use the sigmoid function?", "a": "<p>It maps any real number to (0, 1), is monotonic and differentiable with a convenient derivative σ(1−σ), and is the inverse of the logit, so it follows naturally from modelling log-odds linearly (also the canonical link for the Bernoulli distribution).</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-6558444", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why not use MSE as the loss for logistic regression?", "a": "<p>MSE through a sigmoid is non-convex (local minima) and its gradient is multiplied by σ'(z), which is tiny when the prediction is confidently wrong, so learning stalls. Log-loss is the negative Bernoulli log-likelihood, convex in w, and its gradient (p̂ − y)x is large exactly when the model is wrong.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-c16b38c", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "How do you interpret a logistic regression coefficient?", "a": "<p>A one-unit increase in x_j changes the log-odds by w_j, i.e., multiplies the odds by e^{w_j} (odds ratio), holding other features constant. E.g., w = 0.4 → odds × 1.49 (+49%). The change in probability depends on the baseline probability.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-901404f", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Is the decision boundary of logistic regression linear?", "a": "<p>Yes: p̂ = 0.5 ⇔ wᵀx + b = 0, a hyperplane in feature space (any threshold t gives wᵀx + b = logit(t), still a hyperplane). It becomes non-linear in the original space only if you add non-linear features (polynomials, splines, interactions).</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-f8a706d", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are the assumptions of logistic regression?", "a": "<p>Binary/categorical outcome; independent observations; linearity between continuous features and the log-odds; little multicollinearity; no extreme influential outliers; sufficiently large sample (≈10+ events per predictor). It does not assume normality, homoscedasticity or linear x–y relationships.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-7f77828", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Does logistic regression have a closed-form solution?", "a": "<p>No — setting the gradient Xᵀ(σ(Xw) − y) = 0 gives non-linear equations. It is solved iteratively: Newton–Raphson / IRLS, L-BFGS, coordinate descent (liblinear), or SGD/SAGA. Convexity guarantees a global optimum (if it exists).</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-db9f5cf", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What happens with perfectly separable data?", "a": "<p>The likelihood keeps increasing as ‖w‖ → ∞ (probabilities pushed to exactly 0/1), so the MLE doesn't exist; coefficients and SEs blow up and solvers fail to converge. Fix: regularisation (default L2 in sklearn), Firth's penalised likelihood, or removing the perfectly predictive (often leaking!) feature.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-c489fda", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you handle class imbalance with logistic regression?", "a": "<p>class_weight=\"balanced\" (or custom weights), resampling (undersample majority, SMOTE oversampling inside CV), and — most importantly — moving the decision threshold based on business costs or F-score on a validation set. Evaluate with PR-AUC, recall/precision, not accuracy. Note weighting/resampling biases predicted probabilities; recalibrate if you need true probabilities.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-4ea0f0c", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "One-vs-rest vs multinomial (softmax) logistic regression?", "a": "<p>OvR trains K independent binary models; simple and parallel, but probabilities aren't jointly modelled. Multinomial fits one model with a softmax over K linear scores and cross-entropy loss — coherent probabilities summing to 1, usually better calibrated; it's sklearn's default with lbfgs.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-2943110", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Logistic regression vs linear SVM?", "a": "<p>Both are linear classifiers with L2 regularisation. LR minimises log-loss (every point contributes, gives probabilities); SVM minimises hinge loss (only margin violators / support vectors matter, no probabilities without Platt scaling, maximises the margin). SVM can be more robust to points far from the boundary; LR is better when you need calibrated probabilities.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-b38299f", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Logistic regression vs Naive Bayes?", "a": "<p>LR is discriminative (models P(y|x) directly); NB is generative (models P(x|y)P(y) with a feature-independence assumption). With little data NB converges faster and can win; with more data LR typically achieves lower asymptotic error (Ng & Jordan). In fact Gaussian NB with shared variances implies a linear (logistic-form) decision boundary.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-60e0981", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the log-loss of a model that always predicts 0.5? What's a good log-loss?", "a": "<p>ln 2 ≈ 0.693 for any labels. A useful baseline is predicting the base rate π: loss = −[π ln π + (1−π) ln(1−π)] (e.g., 0.199 for π = 5%). A model is only useful if it beats that baseline.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-d1d354a", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the gradient of the log-loss and how does it compare with linear regression?", "a": "<p>∇L = (1/n) Xᵀ(σ(Xw) − y). It has the same structure as linear regression's Xᵀ(Xw − y) — residual times input — because both are GLMs using their canonical link. The Hessian is XᵀSX with S = diag(p(1−p)), used by Newton/IRLS.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-8dcdc37", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Are logistic regression probabilities calibrated?", "a": "<p>Generally yes when the model is well specified and unweighted, because maximising log-likelihood is a proper scoring rule. Strong regularisation, class weights, resampling or misspecification can miscalibrate it; check with a reliability diagram / Brier score and fix with Platt or isotonic calibration.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-71e5261", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "How would you capture non-linear effects in logistic regression?", "a": "<p>Add polynomial or spline basis functions (SplineTransformer), bin continuous variables (WoE binning in credit scoring), log/ratio transforms, interaction terms, or kernel approximations (Nystroem + LR). If many interactions matter, move to tree ensembles.</p>"}, {"id": "logistic-regression-cab95ed", "p": "logistic-regression.html", "g": "Logistic regression", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Which metric would you use to evaluate a logistic regression model?", "a": "<p>Depends on the goal: ROC-AUC for ranking quality, PR-AUC/F1/recall@precision for rare positives, log-loss/Brier for probability quality, calibration plots when probabilities drive decisions, and a business cost metric at the chosen threshold. Accuracy only with balanced classes and symmetric costs.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-ec1522b", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why is kNN called a lazy learner?", "a": "<p>It does no generalisation during training — it just stores the data. All computation (distance calculations, voting) happens at prediction time. Contrast with eager learners (e.g., logistic regression) that build a model up front and discard the training data.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-a284d54", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "How does the choice of k affect the model?", "a": "<p>Small k → flexible, low bias, high variance (overfits noise; k = 1 has zero training error). Large k → smoother, higher bias, lower variance; k = n predicts the majority class. Choose by cross-validation; prefer odd k for binary problems.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-413d9e7", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why do you need feature scaling for kNN?", "a": "<p>Distances are dominated by features with the largest ranges, so unscaled kNN effectively ignores small-range features. Standardisation or min–max scaling gives each feature comparable influence. Fit the scaler on training data only.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-e1fa505", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the curse of dimensionality and how does it affect kNN?", "a": "<p>In high dimensions data become sparse and pairwise distances concentrate (nearest ≈ farthest), so neighbourhoods are no longer local and the vote is close to random; you need exponentially more data to keep the same density. Mitigate with feature selection, dimensionality reduction (PCA, embeddings) or metric learning.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-e111371", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the time and space complexity of kNN?", "a": "<p>Training O(1) time (O(n·d) memory to store data). Brute-force prediction O(n·d) per query plus O(n log k) to select neighbours. KD-trees/ball trees cut queries to ~O(log n) in low dimensions but degrade toward brute force as d grows; approximate methods (HNSW, FAISS) trade exactness for speed.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-8a557cc", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does kNN handle categorical features and missing values?", "a": "<p>Categoricals need encoding (one-hot) or a suitable metric (Hamming, Gower for mixed types). Missing values must be imputed first (or use a distance that ignores missing coordinates, like sklearn's nan_euclidean used by KNNImputer).</p>"}, {"id": "knn-d73349d", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "kNN for regression — how?", "a": "<p>Predict the (optionally distance-weighted) mean of the k neighbours' targets. Predictions are piecewise constant and can't extrapolate beyond the range of training targets.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-d781a77", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose the distance metric?", "a": "<p>By data type and CV: Euclidean for scaled continuous data, Manhattan for high-dimensional or outlier-prone data, cosine for text/embeddings, Hamming/Jaccard for binary/sets, Mahalanobis for correlated features, Gower for mixed data.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-645ec3a", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the asymptotic error of 1-NN?", "a": "<p>Cover & Hart (1967): as n → ∞, the 1-NN error rate is at most twice the Bayes (optimal) error. With k → ∞ and k/n → 0, kNN is consistent (approaches Bayes error).</p>"}, {"id": "knn-1426756", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "kNN vs K-Means?", "a": "<p>Different problems: kNN is supervised (classification/regression) using k labelled neighbours; K-Means is unsupervised clustering into K groups by iteratively updating centroids. Both rely on distance and need scaling.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-c292aef", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Where is kNN used in practice today?", "a": "<p>Similarity search and recommendations (“customers like you”), vector databases / RAG retrieval with approximate NN, imputation (KNNImputer), anomaly detection (distance to k-th neighbour, LOF), and as a quick baseline on small datasets.</p>"}, {"id": "knn-b888656", "p": "knn.html", "g": "k-Nearest Neighbours", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does kNN deal with imbalanced classes?", "a": "<p>Poorly by default — the majority class tends to win the vote in overlap regions. Use distance weighting, resampling, class-weighted votes, or tune a threshold on the vote fraction (predict_proba).</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-f2e9817", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why is Naive Bayes called “naive”?", "a": "<p>Because it assumes all features are conditionally independent given the class, so P(x₁,…,x_d | y) = ∏ P(x_j | y). This is rarely true (words like “New” and “York” co-occur), but it makes the model tractable with little data.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-366b865", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why does Naive Bayes work well even though the independence assumption is violated?", "a": "<p>Classification only needs the correct class to have the highest score, not accurate probabilities. Dependencies often affect all classes similarly or cancel out, so the argmax is still right even if the probabilities are badly miscalibrated (Domingos & Pazzani). Its low variance also helps on small/high-dimensional data.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-05632c4", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is Laplace smoothing and why is it needed?", "a": "<p>Adding a pseudo-count α (1 for Laplace) to each feature-class count so no conditional probability is zero. Without it, a single unseen word in a test document makes the whole class likelihood zero regardless of all other evidence.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-9c0d22d", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Difference between Multinomial, Bernoulli and Gaussian NB?", "a": "<p>Multinomial models counts/frequencies (how many times each word appears). Bernoulli models binary presence/absence and explicitly penalises absent features — good for short texts. Gaussian models continuous features with a per-class normal distribution.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-df1793e", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Is Naive Bayes generative or discriminative? What does that mean?", "a": "<p>Generative: it models the joint distribution via P(x|y)P(y) and could in principle generate data. Discriminative models (logistic regression, SVM) model P(y|x) or the boundary directly. Generative models need less data and handle missing features naturally; discriminative ones usually win with lots of data.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-bfa5a15", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why do we use log probabilities?", "a": "<p>Multiplying hundreds of probabilities &lt; 1 underflows to 0 in floating point. Log turns products into sums, is monotonic (argmax unchanged), and is numerically stable; normalise with log-sum-exp when you need actual probabilities.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-6ef5cae", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Are Naive Bayes probabilities reliable?", "a": "<p>Not usually — correlated features are counted as independent evidence, pushing probabilities toward 0 or 1. Use NB for ranking/classification; if you need probabilities, calibrate (CalibratedClassifierCV with isotonic or sigmoid).</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-db41a7e", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does Naive Bayes handle continuous and missing features?", "a": "<p>Continuous: GaussianNB, or discretise into bins and use categorical/multinomial NB, or kernel density estimates. Missing: simply drop that feature's term from the sum for that sample (a generative advantage) — though sklearn requires imputation.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-c60af7b", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Show that Multinomial NB is a linear classifier.", "a": "<p>log P(y=1|x) − log P(y=0|x) = log(π₁/π₀) + Σ_j x_j·log(θ_{j1}/θ_{j0}). This is b + wᵀx with w_j = log(θ_{j1}/θ_{j0}), linear in the counts x — the same functional form as logistic regression but with weights estimated generatively rather than by maximising conditional likelihood.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-b4d3637", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you use Naive Bayes over logistic regression?", "a": "<p>Very small training sets, extremely high-dimensional sparse text, need for millisecond training/online updates, or as a quick baseline. With ample data and correlated features, logistic regression or boosting typically perform better.</p>"}, {"id": "naive-bayes-a4160a2", "p": "naive-bayes.html", "g": "Naive Bayes", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Compute P(spam | “free”) given P(spam)=0.2, P(free|spam)=0.5, P(free|ham)=0.05.", "a": "<p>Numerator 0.2 × 0.5 = 0.10; evidence = 0.10 + 0.8 × 0.05 = 0.14; posterior = 0.10 / 0.14 ≈ 0.714.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-74ec7ed", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What are support vectors?", "a": "<p>Training points that lie on the margin boundary or violate it (inside the margin or misclassified) — those with non-zero dual coefficients α. They alone define the hyperplane; removing any other point leaves the solution unchanged.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-b030163", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is the role of C in SVM?", "a": "<p>C is the penalty on slack (margin violations), i.e. inverse regularisation. Large C → fewer violations, narrower margin, risk of overfitting outliers. Small C → wider margin, more violations tolerated, smoother boundary, more bias.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-618fe27", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What does gamma do in an RBF kernel?", "a": "<p>γ controls how far a single training point's influence reaches: K = exp(−γ‖x − z‖²). Large γ → narrow bumps, very wiggly boundary, overfitting; small γ → broad influence, smooth almost-linear boundary, underfitting. Tune jointly with C.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-2251502", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Explain the kernel trick.", "a": "<p>Algorithms that depend on data only through dot products (like the SVM dual) can replace xᵢᵀxⱼ with a kernel K(xᵢ, xⱼ) = φ(xᵢ)ᵀφ(xⱼ). This implicitly operates in a high (even infinite) dimensional feature space where data may be linearly separable, without ever computing φ — cost depends on n, not on the dimension of φ.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-125bd7b", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why maximise the margin?", "a": "<p>A larger margin means the classifier is robust to small perturbations and, from statistical learning theory, has lower capacity (VC-dimension bound depends on margin, not dimension), giving better generalisation guarantees. It also yields a unique solution among all separating hyperplanes.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-d0841bf", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Hinge loss vs log loss — compare SVM and logistic regression.", "a": "<p>Hinge loss is zero for points correctly classified beyond the margin, so only support vectors matter → sparse solution, no probabilities. Log loss is never zero, every point contributes, and it yields calibrated probabilities. Both are convex surrogates for 0-1 loss and use L2 regularisation; performance is often similar for linear problems.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-d8db86c", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why is feature scaling important for SVM?", "a": "<p>The margin is measured in Euclidean distance and the RBF kernel depends on ‖x − z‖, so large-scale features dominate both. Unscaled data also slows down solvers. Standardise inside a pipeline.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-2c797b6", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does SVM handle multiclass classification?", "a": "<p>Via decomposition: one-vs-one (K(K−1)/2 pairwise classifiers, majority vote — sklearn SVC) or one-vs-rest (K classifiers — LinearSVC). Crammer–Singer is a joint multiclass formulation.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-f0194b9", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why solve the dual instead of the primal?", "a": "<p>The dual depends on data only via dot products, enabling kernels; its size depends on n (not on the feature dimension); and the solution is sparse in α (support vectors). For linear SVMs with huge n and moderate d, the primal (LinearSVC, SGD) is actually faster.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-b0d293a", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you not use an SVM?", "a": "<p>Very large datasets (kernel SVM scales ~n²–n³), lots of noise/overlap (many support vectors), when calibrated probabilities or interpretability are needed, or with heterogeneous tabular data where gradient boosting typically wins.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-e5ad7c2", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What makes a function a valid kernel?", "a": "<p>Mercer's condition: K must be symmetric and positive semi-definite — every Gram matrix it produces has non-negative eigenvalues — so it corresponds to an inner product in some feature space. Sums, products and positive scalings of valid kernels are valid kernels.</p>"}, {"id": "svm-ec4d0c2", "p": "svm.html", "g": "Support Vector Machines", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you get probabilities from an SVM?", "a": "<p>Platt scaling: fit a logistic regression on the SVM decision values using cross-validation (SVC(probability=True) or CalibratedClassifierCV). Isotonic regression is an alternative with more data.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-77c7eaf", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "How does a decision tree decide where to split?", "a": "<p>It evaluates every feature and candidate threshold and chooses the split that maximises the impurity decrease (Gini/entropy for classification, variance/MSE reduction for regression), weighted by child sizes. It does this greedily at each node.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-32271a5", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Gini impurity vs entropy — which and why?", "a": "<p>Both measure node impurity and are maximal at a uniform class mix. Gini = 1 − Σp² (no logarithm, slightly faster); entropy = −Σp log p (information-theoretic, slightly more balanced splits). They produce nearly identical trees; Gini is sklearn's default.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-5b79003", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Compute the Gini of a node with 30 positives and 10 negatives.", "a": "<p>p = 0.75, 0.25 → 1 − (0.5625 + 0.0625) = 0.375. Entropy = −0.75 log₂0.75 − 0.25 log₂0.25 ≈ 0.811.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-4739605", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why do decision trees overfit and how do you prevent it?", "a": "<p>They can keep splitting until every leaf is pure, fitting noise. Prevent with pre-pruning (max_depth, min_samples_leaf, min_samples_split, max_leaf_nodes, min_impurity_decrease), post-pruning (cost-complexity ccp_alpha), or by ensembling (bagging/RF, boosting).</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-1aad782", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do regression trees work?", "a": "<p>Same recursive partitioning, but splits minimise the weighted MSE (variance) of children, and leaves predict the mean of their targets (median with MAE). The result is a piecewise-constant function that can't extrapolate.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-b0c7b46", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Do decision trees need feature scaling? Why?", "a": "<p>No. A split only depends on the ordering of a feature's values, which monotonic transformations (scaling, log) preserve. The same goes for tree ensembles.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-58e2832", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What's the difference between pre-pruning and post-pruning?", "a": "<p>Pre-pruning stops growth early with constraints (cheap, but may stop before a useful deeper split — horizon effect). Post-pruning grows the full tree and then removes subtrees whose complexity isn't justified by error reduction, e.g. minimal cost-complexity pruning R(T) + α|T| with α chosen by CV.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-0cea029", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why are decision trees unstable / high variance?", "a": "<p>Greedy hierarchical splits: a small change in data can change the root split, which changes everything below. This is why averaging many trees (bagging, random forests) dramatically reduces variance.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-105275a", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why is impurity-based (MDI) feature importance biased?", "a": "<p>It's computed on training data and favours features with many possible split points (continuous or high-cardinality), which can reduce impurity by chance — even random IDs get importance. Prefer permutation importance on held-out data or SHAP values.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-63644b7", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do trees handle categorical variables and missing values?", "a": "<p>In theory a tree can split a categorical feature into subsets; sklearn needs encoding (ordinal is often fine for trees; one-hot can fragment). LightGBM/CatBoost handle categories natively. Missing values: C4.5 distributes fractionally, CART uses surrogate splits, sklearn ≥ 1.3 / XGBoost / LightGBM learn a default direction for NaNs.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-8224256", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the time complexity of training a decision tree?", "a": "<p>Roughly O(d · n log n) per level with presorting (sort each feature once, then sweep thresholds), giving O(d · n log n · depth) overall; balanced trees have depth ≈ log n. Prediction is O(depth).</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-ec869da", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why can't a tree extrapolate and why does that matter?", "a": "<p>Leaves output constants learned from training targets, so beyond the training range the prediction is flat. For trending data (time series with growth, prices rising) trees underpredict — detrend, model differences, or use linear components.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-1d563f9", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why isn't misclassification error used to grow trees?", "a": "<p>It's piecewise linear (not strictly concave) in class proportions, so many splits that improve purity produce zero change in error and the greedy search gets stuck. Gini and entropy are strictly concave, rewarding any increase in purity.</p>"}, {"id": "decision-trees-50d1e7f", "p": "decision-trees.html", "g": "Decision trees", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How would you explain a decision tree to a business stakeholder?", "a": "<p>Walk a path as a rule: “If tenure &lt; 6 months AND monthly charges &gt; ₹1,500 AND no contract → 72% churn probability (based on 840 customers).” Show the top few levels, leaf sizes and probabilities.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-7821fbb", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "How does a Random Forest work?", "a": "<p>It trains many decision trees, each on a bootstrap sample of the rows, and at every split considers only a random subset of features. Predictions are averaged (regression) or majority-voted / probability-averaged (classification). Bootstrapping + feature subsampling decorrelate trees so averaging reduces variance.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-8d66c52", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why does bagging reduce variance but not bias?", "a": "<p>The average of B identically distributed estimators has the same expectation (so bias is unchanged) but variance ρσ² + (1−ρ)σ²/B, which is smaller than σ². Hence bagging helps high-variance, low-bias learners like deep trees, and does little for stable, high-bias models like linear regression.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-fdb28c1", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why does RF use a random subset of features at each split?", "a": "<p>To decorrelate the trees. Without it, strong predictors dominate the top splits of every tree, making them similar (high ρ), and averaging correlated trees removes little variance. Random subsets force diversity, lowering ρ and the ensemble variance.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-af26503", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Can a Random Forest overfit by adding more trees?", "a": "<p>No — adding trees reduces the variance term and the generalisation error converges to a limit. Individual deep trees overfit, and the forest can still overfit via tree depth/leaf size on noisy data, but n_estimators itself isn't an overfitting knob (it's a compute knob).</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-f23f283", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is the out-of-bag error?", "a": "<p>Each tree leaves out ~36.8% of rows. Predicting each row with only the trees that didn't train on it and scoring those predictions gives an internal, nearly unbiased estimate of test error — like CV for free. Invalid with time ordering or grouped data.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-5e3aa9b", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are the most important RF hyperparameters?", "a": "<p>max_features (controls decorrelation vs tree strength), n_estimators (enough to stabilise), and tree size controls (min_samples_leaf, max_depth). Also max_samples, class_weight for imbalance. RF is fairly insensitive compared with boosting.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-3cc6a0a", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Random Forest vs Gradient Boosting — when would you choose each?", "a": "<p>RF: quick robust baseline, noisy labels, little tuning time, parallel training, lower risk of overfitting. Boosting (XGBoost/LightGBM/CatBoost): usually best accuracy on tabular data when tuned with early stopping, handles bias better, but sequential and more sensitive to hyperparameters and noise.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-2d95780", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why is impurity-based feature importance unreliable?", "a": "<p>It's computed on training data and rewards features offering many split points — continuous or high-cardinality columns (even random IDs) — and it splits credit arbitrarily among correlated features. Use permutation importance on validation data, drop-column importance, or SHAP.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-874c4fe", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Does Random Forest need feature scaling or handle missing values?", "a": "<p>No scaling needed (tree splits are order-based). Missing values: sklearn's RF supports NaN since v1.4; otherwise impute (median + missing-indicator). Categoricals need encoding in sklearn.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-0cdd8c6", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "How do you handle class imbalance in Random Forest?", "a": "<p>class_weight=\"balanced\" or \"balanced_subsample\" (weights recomputed per bootstrap), balanced random forest (undersample majority in each bootstrap — imblearn), threshold tuning on predict_proba, and evaluation with PR-AUC.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-8fb60e7", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does a RF compute predicted probabilities?", "a": "<p>sklearn averages each tree's leaf class proportions (soft voting). These tend to be under-confident near 0/1 (averaging pulls toward the middle); calibrate with isotonic/sigmoid if probabilities matter.</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-2513212", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why are fully grown trees used in RF?", "a": "<p>Averaging fixes variance, not bias, so each base learner should have low bias. Deep trees are low-bias/high-variance — the ideal ingredient. Limiting depth would add bias that averaging can't remove (though min_samples_leaf can help on noisy targets).</p>"}, {"id": "random-forest-5ad8d8e", "p": "random-forest.html", "g": "Bagging & Random Forest", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is ExtraTrees and how is it different?", "a": "<p>Extremely Randomised Trees pick split thresholds at random (choosing the best among random candidates) and by default train on the full dataset (no bootstrap). More randomness → lower variance, faster, slightly more bias.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-8466e49", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is boosting and how does it differ from bagging?", "a": "<p>Boosting trains weak learners sequentially, each focusing on the errors of the current ensemble, and combines them with weights — mainly reducing bias. Bagging trains strong learners independently on bootstrap samples and averages them — mainly reducing variance. Boosting is sequential and can overfit with too many rounds; bagging is parallel and doesn't.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-cc0d226", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Explain how gradient boosting works.", "a": "<p>Initialise with a constant minimising the loss. Repeatedly compute pseudo-residuals = negative gradient of the loss w.r.t. current predictions, fit a small regression tree to them, set optimal leaf values, and add the tree scaled by the learning rate. It's gradient descent in function space; for squared error the pseudo-residuals are the ordinary residuals.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-a90dfca", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why “gradient” boosting? What is the gradient taken with respect to?", "a": "<p>With respect to the model's predictions F(xᵢ) for each training point, not the tree parameters. The vector of negative gradients is the steepest-descent direction in prediction space; each tree approximates that direction as a function of x so it generalises to new points.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-4f5e149", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does AdaBoost work?", "a": "<p>Start with uniform sample weights; fit a weak learner; compute its weighted error ε and weight α = ½ ln((1−ε)/ε); increase weights of misclassified samples by e^{α} and decrease the others, normalise; repeat. The final classifier is the sign of the α-weighted vote. It minimises exponential loss stage-wise.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-4c14803", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why is AdaBoost sensitive to outliers?", "a": "<p>Exponential loss grows exponentially with the negative margin, so persistently misclassified points (mislabelled or outliers) get ever-larger weights and the ensemble contorts itself to fit them. Gradient boosting with log-loss or Huber loss is more robust.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-9f0c7b4", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What is the role of the learning rate in boosting?", "a": "<p>It scales each tree's contribution (shrinkage). Lower values make each step smaller, requiring more trees but reducing overfitting and usually improving test performance. Tune it together with the number of trees via early stopping.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-c4f67d0", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Can gradient boosting overfit? How do you prevent it?", "a": "<p>Yes — with too many rounds, deep trees or a large learning rate it fits noise. Prevent with early stopping on a validation set, lower learning rate, shallower trees / fewer leaves, min samples per leaf, row and column subsampling, and L1/L2 penalties on leaf weights.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-1726620", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why are shallow trees used as base learners in boosting?", "a": "<p>Boosting reduces bias by adding many small corrections; each learner only needs to be slightly better than chance. Shallow trees are low variance, fast, and depth controls the maximum interaction order. Deep trees would overfit quickly and make the sequential correction noisy.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-da65d09", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is the initial prediction F₀ for regression and for binary classification?", "a": "<p>The constant minimising the loss: the mean of y for squared error (median for absolute error) and the log-odds of the positive rate, log(p/(1−p)), for log-loss.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-1c330a6", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is stochastic gradient boosting?", "a": "<p>Friedman's variant: each tree is fit on a random subsample of rows (without replacement, e.g. 50–80%). It injects randomness that reduces variance and correlation between trees, speeds training, and typically improves generalisation. Column subsampling extends the idea.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-ebc5d27", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Gradient boosting vs Random Forest: which handles noisy data better and why?", "a": "<p>Random Forest — averaging independent deep trees is robust, and adding trees doesn't overfit. Boosting keeps fitting residuals, which on noisy labels are largely noise, so it needs careful regularisation and early stopping.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-b89e9e9", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose the number of trees?", "a": "<p>Early stopping: monitor a validation metric each round and stop when it hasn't improved for k rounds (e.g. 50), keeping the best iteration. With CV, take the average best iteration across folds for the final refit on all data.</p>"}, {"id": "boosting-23a05f1", "p": "boosting.html", "g": "AdaBoost & Gradient Boosting", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "How can boosting produce prediction intervals?", "a": "<p>Train models with quantile loss at, say, α = 0.05 and 0.95 (GradientBoostingRegressor(loss=\"quantile\"), LightGBM objective=\"quantile\"), or use conformal prediction on top of any regressor for distribution-free coverage guarantees.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-fc58c90", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Explain XGBoost to a non-technical stakeholder.", "a": "<p>It's a team of simple yes/no rule-trees built one after another. Each new tree studies the mistakes the team is still making and corrects them a little. After hundreds of small corrections the team is very accurate, and built-in limits stop it from memorising quirks in the data.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-87f8759", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you choose XGBoost over LightGBM, and vice versa?", "a": "<p>XGBoost for small-to-medium data where its level-wise, balanced trees overfit less and where its ecosystem features help (monotonic constraints, mature SHAP and Spark support). LightGBM when data is large, from millions of rows up, and training speed and memory dominate, or with very sparse high-dimensional features. On most datasets accuracy is within noise once both are tuned.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-9435ceb", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why can't XGBoost extrapolate, and what do you do about it?", "a": "<p>Every prediction is a sum of leaf values learned from training rows, so outputs stay within the range seen in training. A target that keeps growing (sales trend) gets a flat forecast. Fixes: model the differenced or detrended target, add trend features and predict the residual, or combine with a linear or time-series model.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-02fac2b", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Does XGBoost need feature scaling or one-hot encoding?", "a": "<p>No scaling: trees split on thresholds, so monotonic transforms don't change the result. For categoricals you can one-hot low-cardinality columns, use enable_categorical=True with pandas category dtype, or target-encode carefully. For many high-cardinality categoricals, CatBoost is usually easier.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-671b499", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What makes XGBoost different from classic gradient boosting?", "a": "<p>A regularised objective (γ per leaf, L2/L1 on leaf weights), second-order (Newton) optimisation with gradients and hessians giving closed-form leaf weights and gains, sparsity-aware handling of missing values, weighted quantile sketch / histogram split finding, column subsampling, built-in pruning via γ, and systems work: parallel, cache-aware, out-of-core and GPU training.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-3669d9c", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What do gamma, lambda and min_child_weight do in XGBoost?", "a": "<p>gamma (min_split_loss): minimum gain required to make a split — prunes weak splits. lambda (reg_lambda): L2 penalty on leaf weights, shrinking them toward 0 and appearing in the denominator H + λ. min_child_weight: minimum sum of hessians in a child; blocks tiny or ultra-confident leaves. All three make trees more conservative.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-91c73e5", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does XGBoost handle missing values?", "a": "<p>During training each split tries sending all missing values left and then right and keeps the direction with higher gain — a learned default direction per node. At inference, missing values follow that direction. No imputation needed (though missingness that's informative is captured automatically).</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-89606d8", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Which hyperparameters would you tune first for XGBoost/LightGBM?", "a": "<p>Learning rate + number of trees (via early stopping), then tree complexity (max_depth / num_leaves, min_child_weight / min_child_samples), then sampling (subsample, colsample_bytree), then regularisation (lambda, alpha, gamma). Finish by lowering the learning rate and re-fitting with early stopping.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-cc4041a", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you handle class imbalance in XGBoost?", "a": "<p>scale_pos_weight ≈ negatives/positives (or sample weights), an imbalance-aware eval metric (aucpr) for early stopping, threshold tuning on validation data, and possibly max_delta_step for extreme imbalance with log-loss. Recalibrate probabilities if you reweighted.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-e8edf4e", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Why does XGBoost use the second derivative?", "a": "<p>A second-order approximation gives a Newton step: leaf values account for curvature (−G/H) rather than using a fixed line search, converging faster and more accurately, and it provides a unified closed-form gain for any twice-differentiable loss, making custom objectives easy (just supply g and h).</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-d9c2772", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Which feature importance would you trust from XGBoost?", "a": "<p>Built-in types disagree: weight (# splits) favours continuous features, gain (average loss reduction) is better but still biased, cover is sample-count based. For reliable global and local explanations use SHAP (TreeSHAP), or permutation importance on held-out data.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-998842f", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What are monotonic constraints and why use them?", "a": "<p>They force the model's prediction to be non-decreasing (or non-increasing) in a chosen feature, e.g. credit risk must not decrease as utilisation rises. They encode domain knowledge, improve generalisation/robustness, and satisfy regulators. Set via monotone_constraints in XGBoost/LightGBM.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-a1f4076", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Why do gradient-boosted trees usually beat deep learning on tabular data?", "a": "<p>Tabular features are heterogeneous, often irregular with sharp thresholds and uninformative columns; trees handle mixed scales, missing values and categorical splits natively, are robust to irrelevant features, and need less data and tuning. Neural nets have smooth inductive biases better suited to images, text and audio.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-afb99e2", "p": "xgboost.html", "g": "XGBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "Derive the optimal leaf weight in XGBoost.", "a": "<p>For leaf j, the second-order objective is G_j w + ½(H_j + λ) w² (+ γ). Differentiating and setting to zero: G_j + (H_j + λ) w = 0 ⇒ w* = −G_j / (H_j + λ). Plugging back gives the leaf score −½ G_j²/(H_j + λ), from which the split gain formula follows.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-fd25ac4", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "Why is LightGBM usually faster than XGBoost?", "a": "<p>Four things: it bins features into histograms (and computes one child's histogram by subtraction), it grows trees leaf-wise so it reaches good loss with fewer splits, GOSS can skip many well-predicted rows, and EFB bundles sparse columns. Together these cut both rows and columns scanned. XGBoost's hist mode has closed much of the gap, but LightGBM is still typically faster and lighter on very large data.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-d2eb193", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How do you stop LightGBM overfitting on a small dataset?", "a": "<p>Lower num_leaves (e.g. 15–31) and set max_depth, raise min_child_samples, add subsample/colsample_bytree and L1/L2 regularisation, lower max_bin, use a small learning rate with early stopping, and validate with cross-validation. If it still overfits, XGBoost, CatBoost or Random Forest may simply suit the data better.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-0efc5c5", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What's the relationship between num_leaves and max_depth?", "a": "<p>A balanced tree of depth d has at most 2^d leaves. Because LightGBM grows leaf-wise, num_leaves is the real complexity control. Keep num_leaves below 2^max_depth (e.g. depth 7 → ≤ 127), otherwise trees can become very deep and specific.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-655775a", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does LightGBM handle categorical features?", "a": "<p>Mark them as pandas category (or pass categorical_feature). For each split it sorts categories by their gradient statistics and finds the best cut in that order, giving a many-vs-many partition without one-hot encoding. Regularise rare categories with cat_smooth, min_data_per_group and max_cat_threshold.</p>"}, {"id": "lightgbm-e17f830", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you choose LightGBM over XGBoost or CatBoost?", "a": "<p>Large data (millions of rows or many features), frequent retraining, big hyperparameter searches, sparse high-dimensional inputs, ranking (LambdaRank) or quantile objectives — anywhere training speed and memory are the bottleneck.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-47a1c97", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "Level-wise vs leaf-wise tree growth?", "a": "<p>Level-wise (XGBoost default) splits all nodes at a depth before going deeper — balanced trees, more robust. Leaf-wise (LightGBM) always splits the leaf with the highest gain — reaches lower loss with fewer leaves, faster, but can produce deep unbalanced trees that overfit small data; constrain with num_leaves, min_data_in_leaf and max_depth.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-4e70f62", "p": "lightgbm.html", "g": "LightGBM", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are GOSS and EFB in LightGBM?", "a": "<p>GOSS (Gradient-based One-Side Sampling) keeps all high-gradient (under-fit) rows and a random sample of low-gradient rows, reweighting the sampled ones to keep gain estimates unbiased. EFB (Exclusive Feature Bundling) merges sparse features that are rarely non-zero simultaneously into one, reducing the number of features scanned. Both cut training cost with little accuracy loss.</p>"}, {"id": "catboost-bcc9143", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 1, "q": "What problem does CatBoost solve that other boosters don't?", "a": "<p>Categorical features without manual encoding and without target leakage. Its ordered target statistics encode each row's category using only earlier rows' labels, and ordered boosting computes residuals with models that haven't seen the row.</p>"}, {"id": "catboost-e6c6070", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What is target leakage in target encoding, and how does CatBoost avoid it?", "a": "<p>Naive target encoding replaces a category by its mean target computed on all rows, including the row itself, so the feature contains the answer (extreme for rare categories). The model looks great in training and fails on new data. CatBoost uses a random permutation and only rows before the current one, plus a prior for smoothing, so a row never sees its own label. K-fold target encoding is the manual alternative.</p>"}, {"id": "catboost-a3f8566", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "What are oblivious (symmetric) trees and why use them?", "a": "<p>Trees where every node at a given depth uses the same split. A depth-d tree is d binary tests mapping to 2^d leaves — effectively a lookup table. They're strongly regularised (less overfitting), stable, and extremely fast to evaluate, which suits low-latency serving.</p>"}, {"id": "catboost-390edae", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 3, "q": "What is prediction shift and how does ordered boosting fix it?", "a": "<p>In standard boosting, the residual for a row is computed by a model that was trained on that same row, so residuals on training data are systematically smaller than on new data — the gradient estimates are biased. Ordered boosting keeps models trained on prefixes of a permutation and computes each row's residual with a model that never saw it.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-1e74f40", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "How does CatBoost handle categorical features?", "a": "<p>With ordered target statistics: under a random permutation, each row's category is encoded with the (prior-smoothed) mean target of rows that come before it, so its own label doesn't leak. It also builds combinations of categorical features greedily. Ordered boosting applies the same idea to residual computation to avoid prediction shift.</p>"}, {"id": "xgboost-76218a0", "p": "catboost.html", "g": "CatBoost", "grp": "Supervised models", "l": 2, "q": "When would you pick CatBoost over LightGBM?", "a": "<p>Many (high-cardinality) categorical features, small-to-medium datasets where overfitting is a risk, limited tuning time (strong defaults), or when fast and stable inference matters (symmetric trees). LightGBM for very large datasets and fastest training.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-b3fa619", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "Explain the K-means algorithm.", "a": "<p>Initialise K centroids; assign each point to the nearest centroid; recompute each centroid as the mean of its points; repeat until assignments stop changing. It minimises the within-cluster sum of squared distances (inertia).</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-eefacf4", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Does K-means always converge? To the global optimum?", "a": "<p>It always converges in a finite number of steps because both steps monotonically decrease (never increase) the objective and there are finitely many partitions. But only to a local minimum — results depend on initialisation, hence k-means++ and multiple restarts.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-a7e414a", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "How do you choose K?", "a": "<p>Elbow method on inertia, average silhouette score, Davies–Bouldin / Calinski–Harabasz indices, gap statistic, stability across bootstrap samples, and — often decisive — business interpretability/actionability of the segments.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-6be6522", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What is k-means++ and why is it better?", "a": "<p>An initialisation that picks the first centroid randomly and each subsequent one with probability ∝ squared distance to the nearest chosen centroid. It spreads centroids out, avoids several centroids in one cluster, gives an O(log K) approximation guarantee in expectation, and speeds convergence.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-f471207", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What are the assumptions/limitations of K-means?", "a": "<p>Clusters are spherical, convex, similar in size and density; Euclidean geometry; features on the same scale; K known; sensitive to outliers and initialisation; numeric data only; every point assigned (no noise). Fails on moons/rings, elongated clusters, varied densities.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-fe352d6", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "Why scale features before K-means?", "a": "<p>Euclidean distances are dominated by large-range features, so clusters would be formed almost entirely along those. Standardise (or use domain-meaningful weights) so each feature contributes appropriately.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-e8594d5", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What is the silhouette score?", "a": "<p>For each point: s = (b − a)/max(a, b), where a is its mean distance to points in its own cluster and b its mean distance to points of the nearest other cluster. Ranges −1 to 1: near 1 well clustered, ~0 on a boundary, negative probably misassigned. Average over points to compare K.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-cd87fa5", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How is K-means related to Gaussian Mixture Models?", "a": "<p>K-means is the limit of EM for a GMM with equal, spherical covariances (σ²I, σ → 0) and equal weights, with hard instead of soft assignments. GMMs generalise it: elliptical clusters, different sizes, soft probabilistic memberships.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-3624b85", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How would you cluster categorical data?", "a": "<p>K-modes (matching dissimilarity, modes as centres), K-prototypes for mixed data, hierarchical clustering with Gower distance, or embed categories (e.g. entity embeddings / MCA) and then cluster.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-e74673e", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 3, "q": "How do you evaluate clustering without labels — and with labels?", "a": "<p>Without: internal metrics (silhouette, DB, CH), stability across resamples, and qualitative/business review of segment profiles. With ground truth: Adjusted Rand Index, Normalised/Adjusted Mutual Information, homogeneity/completeness/V-measure.</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-03178c4", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What's the complexity of K-means and how do you scale it?", "a": "<p>O(n·K·d·iterations) time, O((n + K)·d) memory. Scale with MiniBatchKMeans, Elkan's algorithm (triangle inequality), PCA to reduce d, sampling for initialisation, or distributed implementations (Spark MLlib).</p>"}, {"id": "kmeans-2930d9b", "p": "kmeans.html", "g": "K-Means clustering", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Give a business use case and how you'd validate the result.", "a": "<p>Customer segmentation on RFM (recency, frequency, monetary) features: log-transform and scale, choose K with silhouette + marketing input, profile clusters (means, sizes), name them (“lapsed high-value”), check stability over time, and validate by running targeted campaigns (A/B tests) per segment.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-55187eb", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "How does hierarchical clustering work and what's a dendrogram?", "a": "<p>Agglomerative: start with each point as a cluster and repeatedly merge the two closest clusters per the linkage criterion until one remains. The dendrogram is the tree of merges with heights = merge distances; cutting it horizontally gives a flat clustering, and long vertical gaps suggest natural cluster counts.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-e8de2d2", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Compare single, complete, average and Ward linkage.", "a": "<p>Single: min pairwise distance — follows chains, finds non-convex shapes but sensitive to noise. Complete: max distance — compact clusters, outlier-sensitive. Average: mean distance — balanced. Ward: minimises the increase in within-cluster variance — compact, equal-size clusters similar to K-means; requires Euclidean distance.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-d948627", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "Explain DBSCAN and its parameters.", "a": "<p>Density-based clustering: a core point has at least min_samples points within distance eps; clusters grow by connecting core points that are within eps of each other plus their border points; everything else is noise. eps sets the neighbourhood size, min_samples the density threshold. Choose eps from the knee of a k-distance plot.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-b38e992", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "When does DBSCAN fail?", "a": "<p>Clusters with very different densities (one eps can't fit all — use HDBSCAN or OPTICS), high-dimensional data where distances concentrate, unscaled features, and when eps is poorly chosen (too small → everything noise, too large → one cluster).</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-a5eb515", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "K-means vs GMM?", "a": "<p>K-means: hard assignments, spherical equal-variance clusters, minimises inertia, faster. GMM: soft probabilistic assignments, elliptical clusters with their own covariances and weights, maximises likelihood via EM, gives densities and supports BIC model selection. K-means is a limiting case of GMM.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-d18ccf0", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Explain the EM algorithm.", "a": "<p>For models with latent variables: the E-step computes the posterior over latent variables (responsibilities) given current parameters; the M-step maximises the expected complete-data log-likelihood to update parameters. Repeat until convergence. Log-likelihood never decreases; converges to a local optimum, so use multiple inits.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-aab532e", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How does Isolation Forest detect anomalies?", "a": "<p>It builds random trees by picking a random feature and a random split value between its min and max. Anomalies are few and different, so they get isolated near the root — short average path lengths across trees. The anomaly score normalises the average path length by that expected for a random point (c(n)).</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-9e53f74", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What is Local Outlier Factor?", "a": "<p>LOF compares a point's local reachability density to that of its k nearest neighbours. Points in a region much sparser than their neighbours' regions get LOF ≫ 1. It finds local outliers in datasets with clusters of different densities, unlike global distance rules.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-615f8d1", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How would you build an anomaly detection system for transactions without labels?", "a": "<p>Engineer behavioural features (amount vs user's history, velocity, new device/merchant, time-of-day), scale, fit an unsupervised detector (Isolation Forest / LOF / autoencoder), set the threshold by alert capacity (top-k per day), have analysts review alerts, and use their feedback as labels to move to a supervised model (with the unsupervised score as a feature). Monitor drift and alert precision.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-d19251d", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 3, "q": "How do you choose the number of components in a GMM?", "a": "<p>Fit over a range of K (and covariance types) and pick the lowest BIC = −2 log L + p log n (AIC uses 2p, tends to choose more components). Also check stability, cluster sizes and interpretability; a Bayesian GMM with a Dirichlet process prior can shrink unused components automatically.</p>"}, {"id": "clustering-fa7f682", "p": "clustering.html", "g": "Hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM & anomalies", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Difference between outlier detection and novelty detection?", "a": "<p>Outlier detection: training data contain outliers; find them in that data (unsupervised). Novelty detection: train on clean normal data only, then flag new observations that differ (semi-supervised), e.g. One-Class SVM or LOF(novelty=True).</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-3412d52", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "What is PCA and how does it work?", "a": "<p>An unsupervised linear transformation to new orthogonal axes (principal components) ordered by the variance they capture. Centre/standardise the data, compute the covariance matrix (or SVD), take eigenvectors sorted by eigenvalue, and project onto the top k to reduce dimensions while retaining most variance.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-171ccbf", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 1, "q": "Why do we standardise data before PCA?", "a": "<p>PCA maximises variance, which depends on units. A feature measured in large units dominates PC1 regardless of its information content. Standardising gives every feature unit variance (PCA on the correlation matrix). Skip only when features share units and their raw variance matters.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-db89f03", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "What do eigenvalues and eigenvectors represent in PCA?", "a": "<p>Eigenvectors of the covariance matrix are the principal directions (loadings); eigenvalues are the variances of the data along those directions. Eigenvalue / sum of eigenvalues = explained variance ratio.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-0a121f2", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How is PCA related to SVD?", "a": "<p>For centred X = USVᵀ, the columns of V are the principal directions, eigenvalues λ = s²/(n−1), and the PC scores are US. Libraries use SVD because it's more numerically stable than forming XᵀX, and randomized SVD scales to big data.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-2f2f740", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose the number of principal components?", "a": "<p>Cumulative explained variance threshold (e.g. 95%), scree-plot elbow, Kaiser criterion, or — for predictive tasks — cross-validate n_components inside a pipeline with the downstream model.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-bad5238", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Are principal components correlated? Interpretable?", "a": "<p>Scores of different PCs are uncorrelated (orthogonal directions, diagonal covariance). Interpretation is hard because each PC mixes all original features; inspect loadings, or use sparse PCA / factor analysis with rotation for interpretability.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-aae924c", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Can PCA hurt model performance?", "a": "<p>Yes. PCA ignores the target, so it may discard low-variance directions that are highly predictive; it also loses interpretability. Tree models rarely benefit. Validate with CV rather than assuming.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-ea588e4", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "PCA vs LDA?", "a": "<p>PCA: unsupervised, maximises total variance, up to d components. LDA: supervised, maximises between-class vs within-class variance ratio, up to C − 1 components, assumes Gaussian classes with shared covariance. Use LDA when the goal is class separation.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-3cdd80e", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "t-SNE vs PCA — when do you use each?", "a": "<p>PCA: linear, deterministic, preserves global variance, fast, can transform new data — preprocessing and compression. t-SNE: non-linear, stochastic, preserves local neighbourhoods, slow, no out-of-sample transform — 2-D visualisation only. Don't read distances between t-SNE clusters or their sizes.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-0d8d911", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 3, "q": "What is the perplexity in t-SNE?", "a": "<p>A smooth measure of the effective number of neighbours each point considers (2^entropy of its neighbour distribution). Low perplexity emphasises very local structure (many small clumps); high perplexity considers broader neighbourhoods. Typical 5–50; try several values.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-c5b479a", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 3, "q": "How is PCA related to linear autoencoders?", "a": "<p>A linear autoencoder with a k-unit bottleneck trained on squared reconstruction error learns a subspace equal to the span of the top-k principal components (though not necessarily the orthogonal PC basis). Non-linear autoencoders generalise PCA.</p>"}, {"id": "dim-reduction-36b6c47", "p": "dim-reduction.html", "g": "PCA & dimensionality reduction", "grp": "Unsupervised", "l": 2, "q": "Does PCA help with multicollinearity in regression?", "a": "<p>Yes — principal component regression uses uncorrelated PCs as predictors, removing collinearity, at the cost of interpretability and possibly dropping predictive low-variance directions. Ridge regression is a soft version that shrinks low-variance directions most.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-6ccaaca", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 1, "q": "What is stationarity and why does it matter?", "a": "<p>A weakly stationary series has constant mean and variance over time and autocovariance that depends only on lag. Models like ARMA assume it so that patterns learned from the past apply to the future; non-stationary series can produce spurious correlations and unreliable forecasts. Check with plots, ADF and KPSS; fix with differencing or transforms.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-f02a434", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How do you choose p, d, q for ARIMA?", "a": "<p>d: number of differences needed for stationarity (ADF/KPSS). p: lag where the PACF cuts off (AR order). q: lag where the ACF cuts off (MA order). Refine by AIC/BIC grid search (auto_arima) and verify residuals are white noise (Ljung–Box).</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-fb71d12", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 1, "q": "Why can't you use regular K-fold cross-validation for time series?", "a": "<p>Shuffling puts future observations in training and past ones in validation, leaking future information and producing over-optimistic scores; autocorrelated neighbours also leak. Use time-ordered splits (expanding/sliding window, TimeSeriesSplit), with a gap for look-back features.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-058a053", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "ACF vs PACF?", "a": "<p>ACF at lag k is the correlation between y_t and y_{t−k}, including indirect effects through intermediate lags. PACF at lag k is the correlation after controlling for lags 1…k−1 — the direct effect. AR(p) shows PACF cut-off at p; MA(q) shows ACF cut-off at q.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-3788dda", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How would you forecast sales for 10,000 products?", "a": "<p>A global model (LightGBM) trained across all series with lag/rolling/calendar/price/promo features and product/store categoricals, predicting scaled or differenced targets; hierarchical reconciliation if forecasts must sum across levels; special handling for intermittent and new (cold-start) products; rolling-origin backtests; WAPE/MASE metrics; baselines per series.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-4e434d0", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "What are the pitfalls of MAPE?", "a": "<p>Undefined/infinite when actuals are zero, explodes for small actuals, asymmetric (over-forecasts can be penalised without bound while under-forecasts cap at 100%), and it favours models that under-forecast. Alternatives: WAPE, sMAPE, MASE.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-b46e92f", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "Recursive vs direct multi-step forecasting?", "a": "<p>Recursive: one 1-step model, feed predictions back as inputs — simple but errors compound. Direct: separate model per horizon — no compounding but more models and ignores dependencies between horizons. Hybrids and multi-output models sit in between.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-f5b95c3", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How do tree models handle trend in time series?", "a": "<p>Poorly — they can't extrapolate beyond the training target range. Remove trend first (differencing, ratio to a rolling mean, detrending with a linear model) and let the trees model the stationary remainder, or combine a linear trend component with tree residual models.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-db50571", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 3, "q": "What is a unit root?", "a": "<p>In y_t = ρ y_{t−1} + ε_t, ρ = 1 is a unit root: a random walk whose shocks persist forever and variance grows with time — non-stationary. ADF tests H₀: unit root. First differencing turns a random walk into stationary white noise.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-386fa8c", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How do you handle multiple seasonalities?", "a": "<p>Fourier terms for each period as regressors (e.g. daily + weekly + yearly), TBATS, Prophet (multiple seasonal components), MSTL decomposition, or calendar features in ML models.</p>"}, {"id": "time-series-38b2fd7", "p": "time-series.html", "g": "Time-series forecasting", "grp": "Applied", "l": 2, "q": "How do you produce prediction intervals?", "a": "<p>From model-based standard errors (ARIMA/ETS), quantile regression (LightGBM quantile objective / pinball loss), bootstrapped residual simulation, or conformal prediction with time-aware calibration. Evaluate with coverage and interval width.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-396a10f", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 1, "q": "What is MLOps and why is it needed?", "a": "<p>The set of practices to reliably build, deploy, monitor and improve ML systems in production — combining ML, data engineering and DevOps. It's needed because models depend on changing data, fail silently, must be reproducible and auditable, and need retraining; without automation, models rot and releases are slow and risky.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-4551e9b", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 1, "q": "What is the difference between data drift and concept drift?", "a": "<p>Data (covariate) drift: the distribution of inputs P(X) changes (e.g. a new customer demographic). Concept drift: the relationship between inputs and target P(Y|X) changes (e.g. fraudsters adapt, so the same features now mean something else). Data drift is detectable without labels; concept drift usually needs labels or proxy signals.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-8babe50", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How do you detect drift in production?", "a": "<p>Compare production windows against a reference (training or a stable period): per-feature statistics and tests (PSI, KS, χ², Jensen–Shannon/Wasserstein), prediction-distribution monitoring, multivariate detectors (domain classifier: train a model to distinguish reference vs production — AUC ≫ 0.5 means drift), data-quality checks, and performance metrics once labels arrive. Alert on effect-size thresholds, not just p-values.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-229cf22", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What should you monitor for a deployed model?", "a": "<p>Service health (latency percentiles, errors, throughput, resources), data quality (schema, nulls, ranges, new categories, freshness, volume), input and prediction drift, model performance on labelled feedback (overall and by segment), business KPIs, fairness metrics, and cost.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-064b4cf", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What is training–serving skew and how do you prevent it?", "a": "<p>A mismatch between the data/features the model saw in training and what it receives in production — different code paths, data sources, timing or preprocessing. Prevent with a feature store (shared definitions, point-in-time correct offline data), packaging preprocessing inside the model artifact, logging serving features and training on them, and skew monitors comparing online vs offline features.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-92cf9c6", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "Batch vs real-time inference — how do you choose?", "a": "<p>Batch when predictions can be precomputed and a delay is acceptable (daily churn scores) — cheaper and simpler. Real-time when the decision depends on fresh context at request time (fraud at checkout, search ranking) — needs low-latency serving, online features and higher operational cost. Streaming in between for continuous event-driven scoring.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-e1cf367", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "Explain shadow, canary and blue–green deployments.", "a": "<p>Shadow: new model gets a copy of live traffic but its predictions aren't used — safe validation. Canary: a small share of real traffic goes to the new model, ramped up if metrics hold. Blue–green: two full environments; traffic switches from blue (old) to green (new) at once, enabling instant rollback.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-aad0311", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What is a feature store?", "a": "<p>A centralised system for defining, computing, storing and serving features: an offline store (historical values with point-in-time joins for training) and an online store (low-latency latest values for inference), with metadata, sharing/reuse across teams, lineage and monitoring. Examples: Feast, Tecton, Databricks, Vertex AI, SageMaker Feature Store.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-c9160cc", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What does a model registry provide?", "a": "<p>Versioned storage of model artifacts with metadata (metrics, parameters, training data and code versions), lifecycle stages/aliases (staging, production, archived), approval workflows, lineage, and a single source of truth that deployment pipelines pull from — enabling reproducibility, audits and rollbacks.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-557f857", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How do you decide when to retrain a model?", "a": "<p>Based on how fast the domain changes and the cost of staleness vs retraining: scheduled retraining matched to the drift rate, triggered retraining on drift/performance alerts, or online learning for fast-moving data. Use backtests to estimate how performance decays with model age, and always gate new models with validation and canaries.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-d749a7d", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How do you ensure reproducibility of ML experiments?", "a": "<p>Version code (Git), data (snapshots/DVC/hashes) and environment (pinned dependencies, Docker); fix random seeds; log parameters, metrics and artifacts with experiment tracking; parameterise pipelines with configs; record lineage in the model registry.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-1682bd6", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 3, "q": "Your model's accuracy dropped suddenly in production. Walk through your investigation.", "a": "<p>Verify the alert (label delay, metric bug). Find the start time and correlate with deployments and upstream changes. Check data quality (schema, nulls, units, broken joins, stale features). Measure feature and prediction drift and per-segment performance. Compare online vs offline features for skew. Mitigate (rollback/fallback/fix pipeline), retrain if concept drift, then add monitors/tests and write a post-mortem.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-ad41255", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "What tests would you put in an ML CI pipeline?", "a": "<p>Unit tests for feature transformations; data validation (schema, ranges, null rates, distribution expectations); training smoke test on a small sample; model quality gates vs the current champion on a fixed holdout and critical slices; invariance/directional tests (e.g. increasing income shouldn't increase default risk); latency and artifact-size checks; fairness checks; API contract tests.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-8797829", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you handle delayed ground-truth labels when monitoring?", "a": "<p>Monitor leading indicators: input drift, prediction-distribution drift, data quality, business proxies (e.g. early repayment signals). Estimate performance with calibrated confidence (CBPE). Build pipelines that join labels when they arrive and backfill performance dashboards; set retraining cadence around label availability.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-f3cef22", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 3, "q": "What are feedback loops in ML systems and how do you mitigate them?", "a": "<p>When predictions influence the data the model is later trained on — recommendations shape clicks; credit models only observe outcomes for approved applicants; predictive policing. They bias training data and can amplify errors. Mitigate with exploration (randomised holdouts, bandits), logging propensities and using inverse-propensity weighting, reject inference, and monitoring diversity/coverage.</p>"}, {"id": "mlops-7de8cd0", "p": "mlops.html", "g": "MLOps & production ML", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you serve a model with very tight latency (e.g. &lt; 10 ms)?", "a": "<p>Precompute heavy features and cache them in an online store; use a lightweight model or distil/quantise a big one; export to an optimised runtime (ONNX Runtime, Treelite for trees); keep the model in memory in a compiled service; batch where possible; colocate with callers; autoscale; set timeouts with a fallback (default score or rules).</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-3cc2946", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you design a system to detect fake reviews?", "a": "<p>Clarify the action (hide, downrank, review) and costs. Labels from moderation decisions and known fraud rings (noisy; use active learning). Features: text (embeddings, repetitiveness, sentiment extremity), reviewer behaviour (account age, review velocity, rating variance, burstiness on one product), graph signals (reviewer–product bipartite communities), metadata (IP/device sharing, verified purchase). Model: GBM on behavioural + text features, graph-based detection for rings. Metrics: precision at moderation capacity, recall on known rings; monitor adversarial shifts; human review loop.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-2a65492", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How do you define the label for a churn model?", "a": "<p>Work with the business: explicit cancellation for subscriptions; for non-contractual businesses, inactivity beyond a threshold chosen from the return-probability curve (e.g. 95% of users inactive for 45 days never return). Fix a prediction point and horizon (churn within 30 days after snapshot), exclude already-churned users, and keep feature windows strictly before the snapshot.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-3cae099", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you handle the cold-start problem in recommendations?", "a": "<p>New users: popular/trending items, onboarding questions, contextual signals (location, device, referrer), session-based recommendations that adapt within the first clicks. New items: content-based features/embeddings (text, image, category, price), exploration boosts or bandits to gather interactions, similar-item mapping.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-7f16aa5", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 3, "q": "How would you evaluate a recommender offline and online?", "a": "<p>Offline: time-split holdout of interactions; recall@k for candidate generation; NDCG@k/MAP/MRR for ranking; coverage, diversity, novelty; counterfactual/off-policy evaluation with logged propensities (IPS) to correct exposure bias. Online: A/B test on CTR, conversion, revenue/session, retention, with guardrails (latency, diversity, complaints).</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-29df504", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "Batch-scored model or real-time API for a credit-limit increase campaign?", "a": "<p>Batch: eligibility and offers are decided periodically, latency isn't critical, features come from monthly statements, and batch is cheaper, easier to audit and explain. Real-time only if the offer is triggered by in-session events.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-7588906", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "How would you predict ETA for a food delivery app?", "a": "<p>Decompose into stages (restaurant prep time, courier assignment & pickup, travel time) with separate models or a model with stage features; features: restaurant historical prep times by hour, current order load, courier location & availability, route distance, real-time traffic, weather; GBM with quantile loss for P50/P90; evaluate MAE and on-time %; online calibration; monitor during peaks and adjust quickly with real-time features.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-215d807", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 3, "q": "How would you ensure fairness in a loan approval model?", "a": "<p>Exclude protected attributes as inputs but test for proxies; measure fairness metrics across groups (approval-rate parity, equal opportunity/TPR parity, calibration within groups); analyse trade-offs with business and legal; mitigations: reweighting, constraints during training, group-specific thresholds where legal; explainability (reason codes, adverse-action notices); documentation (model cards), monitoring and periodic audits.</p>"}, {"id": "system-design-34699f0", "p": "system-design.html", "g": "ML system design", "grp": "Production", "l": 2, "q": "Your model will be used to rank search results. Which loss and metrics?", "a": "<p>Learning-to-rank: pointwise (regression/classification of relevance), pairwise (RankNet, LambdaRank) or listwise (LambdaMART, ListNet). LambdaMART (LightGBM/XGBoost rank:ndcg) is a strong default. Offline NDCG@10/MRR from graded relevance labels or click models (correct position bias); online CTR, successful sessions, time to click, abandonment.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-a483089", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "1. Your loss is not converging — it keeps wandering up and down — even though the learning rate is already small. Why?", "a": "<p>A small learning rate rules out only one cause (overshooting). The usual remaining suspects, in the order I'd check them:</p><ol><li><b>Gradient noise from small or unshuffled batches.</b> With a constant step, SGD never settles; it hovers at a noise floor proportional to learning rate × gradient variance. Fix: larger batch, learning-rate decay, or averaging the weights.</li><li><b>Unscaled features / ill-conditioned loss.</b> One direction is steep and another almost flat, so any single step size is too big for one and too small for the other. Fix: standardise inputs, use Adam or momentum.</li><li><b>Data problems:</b> label noise, outliers, NaNs/infs, wrong labels in some batches — each bad batch kicks the weights.</li><li><b>Bugs:</b> not zeroing gradients, wrong loss sign, applying softmax twice, shuffling features and labels separately, a learning-rate schedule that resets (warm restarts).</li><li><b>A non-stationary or impossible target:</b> features carry no signal, or the loss is non-convex with a noisy plateau.</li></ol><p><b>How I'd diagnose:</b> plot a smoothed loss; try to overfit one tiny batch (if that fails it's a bug); switch to full-batch for a few steps (if it becomes smooth it was noise); check gradient norms and input ranges.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-72cd262", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "2. Training loss goes down for a while and then suddenly becomes NaN. What do you do?", "a": "<p>NaN means a number overflowed or an invalid operation happened. Causes: learning rate too high so weights explode; exploding gradients (deep or recurrent nets); log(0) or division by zero in the loss (probabilities exactly 0/1); invalid inputs (NaN/inf in features, negative values into log or sqrt); mixed-precision overflow.</p><p><b>Steps:</b> find the first step that produced NaN and inspect that batch; assert inputs are finite; lower the learning rate 10×; add gradient clipping; use numerically stable losses (logits + log-sum-exp instead of probability then log; add epsilon/clipping); check normalisation layers and weight initialisation. If it happens only on particular rows, it's a data problem, not an optimiser problem.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-1f7c774", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "3. The loss decreases extremely slowly. How do you speed up convergence?", "a": "<p>First decide whether it's slow optimisation or a model that has nothing more to learn (compare with a baseline and with the validation curve).</p><ul><li><b>Learning rate too low</b> → run a learning-rate range test and pick the largest stable value; use a schedule with warm-up.</li><li><b>Poor conditioning</b> → standardise features, use Adam/momentum, batch normalisation.</li><li><b>Saturated activations / vanishing gradients</b> → ReLU-family activations, better initialisation (He/Xavier), residual connections.</li><li><b>Too much regularisation</b> (large weight decay, dropout) holding the model back.</li><li><b>For boosted trees:</b> learning rate so small that thousands of trees are needed — raise it, tune structure, and lower it again only at the end.</li><li><b>Data loader bottleneck</b> — it's wall-clock slow, not optimisation slow; profile it.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "situational-0025086", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "4. Training loss is stuck at exactly the same value from the first epoch (e.g. 0.693 for a binary classifier). What does that tell you?", "a": "<p>0.693 = ln 2 is the loss of predicting 0.5 for everything: the model is learning nothing. Typical causes: gradients aren't reaching the weights (parameters frozen, optimiser given the wrong parameter list, <code>zero_grad</code>/<code>step</code> in the wrong place, detached tensors); dead ReLUs after a bad initialisation or too-high learning rate; labels shuffled relative to features; all-constant or all-zero inputs after a preprocessing bug; learning rate effectively 0; the target has no relationship to the features.</p><p><b>Check:</b> can it overfit 10 examples? Are gradient norms non-zero? Do weights change between steps? Print a few (x, y) pairs after the full pipeline to confirm they still match.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-637421b", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "5. Training loss oscillates wildly from the very start. What's the first thing you change?", "a": "<p>Lower the learning rate — oscillation from step one is the classic sign that steps overshoot the minimum (on a quadratic, any step above 2/curvature diverges, and above 1/curvature zig-zags). Then: standardise the inputs so curvature is similar in all directions; increase batch size to reduce gradient noise; add gradient clipping; use warm-up so early large gradients don't throw the weights far away; with Adam, check epsilon and that weight decay is not huge. Confirm by plotting loss against learning rate in a range test: choose a value about 10× below where the loss starts to blow up.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-7e7242e", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "6. Your training loss shows a regular saw-tooth pattern that repeats every epoch. What is happening?", "a": "<p>The data aren't shuffled between epochs (or are sorted by class, date or source). Each stretch of the epoch contains a different kind of example, so the model adapts to that chunk, then partly forgets it when the next chunk arrives — the same pattern repeats every pass. Other causes: a cyclical learning-rate schedule, or the last, smaller batch of each epoch having a different loss. Fix: shuffle every epoch (and across shards, not only within a shard), stratify batches if classes are rare, and check the logging isn't mixing per-batch and per-epoch averages.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-dab2a31", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "7. Validation loss is lower than training loss. Is something wrong?", "a": "<p>Not necessarily. Innocent explanations: dropout and other regularisers are active in training but switched off at evaluation; training loss is averaged over the epoch while weights were still improving, validation is measured at the end; data augmentation makes training examples harder; the validation set happens to be easier or is small.</p><p>Worrying explanations: leakage (duplicates of training rows in validation), a validation set that isn't representative (different period or segment), or a bug such as evaluating on a subset. Check by evaluating the training set in evaluation mode — if that is now lower than validation, it was just the regularisation/averaging effect.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-64cccc8", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "8. You double the batch size and the model gets worse. Why, and what do you adjust?", "a": "<p>Larger batches give less noisy gradients and fewer updates per epoch. Two effects follow: the same learning rate now makes less progress per epoch, and the model loses the regularising effect of gradient noise, tending toward sharper minima that generalise slightly worse. Adjust: scale the learning rate up roughly in proportion to batch size (with warm-up), train for more epochs or the same number of <i>updates</i>, and consider a bit more explicit regularisation. Also check batch-norm statistics and that the learning-rate schedule is defined in steps, not epochs.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-8ba0eb1", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "9. Gradient descent on your linear model takes thousands of iterations, but a colleague's converges in fifty on the same data. What did they probably do?", "a": "<p>They standardised the features. With features on very different scales the loss surface is a long narrow valley (large condition number): the learning rate must be small enough for the steepest direction, which makes progress along the flat direction painfully slow, and plain gradient descent zig-zags. After scaling, the contours are close to circular and the gradient points almost straight at the minimum. Other possibilities: they used a second-order or quasi-Newton solver (L-BFGS), momentum/Adam, a better learning rate, or simply the closed-form solution.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-104de43", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 3, "q": "10. Two runs with identical code and data give noticeably different results. How do you handle this?", "a": "<p>Sources of randomness: weight initialisation, data shuffling, train/validation split, dropout, bootstrap/column sampling in tree ensembles, non-deterministic GPU operations and multi-threading. First, fix seeds everywhere and make the split deterministic to check whether the difference disappears. Then treat the remaining spread as information: report mean ± standard deviation over several seeds, and don't claim an improvement smaller than seed-to-seed variation. If variance is large, the model is unstable — more data, stronger regularisation, more trees, or averaging several seeds (a cheap ensemble) will help. For comparisons, use the same folds and paired tests.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-9f63a14", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 1, "q": "11. Training accuracy is 99% and test accuracy is 72%. Walk me through what you do.", "a": "<p>That gap is high variance — or leakage/mismatch, so I check those first: is the test set from the same distribution and time period? Any preprocessing fitted on all data? Duplicates across the split?</p><p>If it's genuine overfitting, in order of payoff: get more (or augmented) training data; simplify the model (shallower trees, fewer features, higher <code>min_samples_leaf</code>); add regularisation (L1/L2, dropout, lower learning rate with early stopping, subsampling); remove noisy or ID-like features; use bagging/ensembles; tune by cross-validation instead of a single split. I'd plot a learning curve — if validation is still rising with data size, more data is the best investment.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-f1ee9e6", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 1, "q": "12. Both training and validation error are high. What now?", "a": "<p>High bias: the model can't even fit the data it has seen. Options: add informative features or interactions (usually the biggest win); use a more flexible model (linear → gradient boosting); reduce regularisation; train longer / more trees; fix data issues that cap performance (label noise, wrong target definition, features that don't contain the signal). I'd also estimate the best achievable error — human performance or agreement between labellers — because if the problem is inherently noisy, both errors being “high” may already be near the ceiling. More data will <i>not</i> help a high-bias model.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-49166c7", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "13. You added more training data and performance did not improve. Why?", "a": "<ul><li>The model is <b>bias-limited</b>: learning curves had already flattened, so it needs more capacity or better features, not more rows.</li><li>The new data is <b>more of the same</b> — it doesn't cover the cases the model gets wrong.</li><li>The new data is <b>noisier or from a different distribution</b> (another source, period or labelling standard).</li><li>You're near the <b>irreducible error</b> for these features.</li><li>Hyperparameters tuned for the smaller dataset (regularisation, tree depth, number of iterations) are now wrong.</li></ul><p>Next step: error analysis to see what kind of examples fail, then collect <i>those</i> (active learning), or add features.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-e2ed4db", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "14. Cross-validation says 90%, the held-out test set says 80%. How do you explain the gap?", "a": "<p>Something made the CV estimate optimistic or the test set different:</p><ul><li><b>Leakage inside CV:</b> scaling, imputation, feature selection, target encoding or oversampling done before splitting.</li><li><b>Wrong split type:</b> random folds on grouped or time-ordered data, so near-duplicates sit on both sides.</li><li><b>Tuning optimism:</b> the reported CV score is the best of many configurations.</li><li><b>Distribution shift:</b> the test set is from a later period or different population.</li><li><b>Small test set:</b> the difference is within sampling error — compute a confidence interval.</li></ul><p>I'd rebuild everything inside a pipeline, use group/time-aware splits, and run nested CV to get an honest number.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-0bf3445", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "15. Your model does well on the test set but poorly on new data collected next month. Why?", "a": "<p>The test set shared something with training that next month's data doesn't: the same time period (so no temporal drift), the same customers/devices, the same data pipeline. Causes: concept or data drift, seasonality the training window never saw, a feature that was only available retrospectively (leakage), or training–serving skew in how features are computed live. Fix the evaluation first — hold out the most recent period and unseen entities so the offline number predicts the future — then add drift monitoring, retrain on a recent window, and prefer features that are stable over time.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-33db6df", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "16. A very deep decision tree gets 100% training accuracy. Your manager is pleased. What do you say?", "a": "<p>That 100% on training data mostly proves the tree has memorised it: an unrestricted tree keeps splitting until every leaf is pure, including noise and mislabelled rows. The only number that matters is performance on data it hasn't seen. I'd show the cross-validated score, then control complexity — <code>max_depth</code>, <code>min_samples_leaf</code>, cost-complexity pruning chosen by CV — or move to a random forest or gradient boosting, which keep the flexibility while averaging away the variance. I'd also check for an ID-like or leaky feature that makes perfect separation suspiciously easy.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-00005dc", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "17. Regularisation made your model worse. How is that possible?", "a": "<p>Regularisation trades variance for bias; it only helps when variance was the problem. If the model was already underfitting, or the penalty is too strong, you add bias and lose accuracy. Other causes: features weren't standardised, so the penalty hits coefficients unevenly; the intercept was penalised; the regularisation strength wasn't tuned by cross-validation; with Lasso, a group of correlated useful features was collapsed to one arbitrary survivor. Check the train vs validation error before and after: if both rose, it's too much bias — reduce the penalty.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-341d5a4", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "18. You have only 500 labelled rows and 2,000 features. How do you build a model that generalises?", "a": "<p>p ≫ n is a recipe for overfitting, so everything is about reducing variance and getting an honest estimate:</p><ul><li>Simple, strongly regularised models: Lasso / Elastic Net logistic regression, linear SVM, or a small random forest.</li><li>Reduce dimensionality with domain knowledge, univariate filters, or PCA — <i>inside</i> the CV loop.</li><li>Repeated stratified or nested cross-validation; report a confidence interval.</li><li>Transfer learning or pretrained embeddings if the data are text/images.</li><li>Get more labels where the model is uncertain (active learning), or use semi-supervised methods.</li></ul><p>And never select features on the full dataset before cross-validating — that alone can turn pure noise into “90% accuracy”.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-50bbfb0", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "19. Your model's error is fine on average but terrible for one customer segment. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Averages hide slices. First measure: per-segment metrics with confidence intervals and sample sizes. Then find the cause: the segment is under-represented in training; it behaves differently (a different relationship between features and target); its data quality is worse (more missing values, different source system); or its labels are noisier.</p><p>Remedies: collect or up-weight data for that segment; add features that describe it or interaction terms; train a segment-specific model or let a tree model split on the segment; set segment-specific thresholds; if it can't be fixed quickly, route that segment to a fallback rule or human review. Add the slice to the standing evaluation so it can't regress silently.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-7994818", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "20. How do you decide between collecting more data and building a more complex model?", "a": "<p>Plot a learning curve (score against training-set size). If training and validation scores have converged at an unsatisfactory level, the model is bias-limited — more data won't help; add features or capacity. If there's a large gap and the validation curve is still rising, it's variance-limited — more data (or regularisation) will help, and you can extrapolate roughly how much. Then weigh costs: labelling cost and time versus engineering and serving cost of a bigger model. Error analysis decides <i>which</i> data: more examples of the failing cases beat more random rows.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-e9e09fd", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 1, "q": "21. 40% of the values in an important feature are missing. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Don't drop it reflexively. First ask <i>why</i> it's missing: at random, dependent on other columns, or dependent on its own value (e.g. income withheld by high earners)? Often the missingness itself is predictive.</p><ul><li>Add a <b>missing-indicator</b> column, then impute (median for a skewed numeric, a “Missing” category for a categorical).</li><li>Try model-based imputation (KNN / iterative) if other columns predict it well.</li><li>Use a model that handles NaN natively (XGBoost, LightGBM, HistGradientBoosting).</li><li>Check whether it will also be missing at prediction time, at the same rate.</li></ul><p>Fit the imputer on training folds only, and compare options by cross-validation instead of guessing.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-847f123", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "22. One feature gives your model 0.99 AUC. What's your reaction?", "a": "<p>Suspicion before celebration: it's probably target leakage. I'd ask how and <i>when</i> that feature is recorded. Typical culprits: a field updated after the outcome (“account_closed_date” for churn, “chargeback_flag” for fraud), a proxy created from the label, an ID or timestamp that encodes how the data was collected, or post-treatment information. Tests: is the feature available at the moment of prediction in production? Does performance survive a strict point-in-time rebuild of the training set? If it's leakage, remove it; if it's genuinely available in advance, great — but then the problem may not need ML at all, just that rule.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-62246f0", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "23. You suspect that many training labels are wrong. How do you proceed?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Measure it:</b> re-label a random sample (ideally by two people) to estimate the noise rate and whether errors are random or systematic.</li><li><b>Find the bad ones:</b> rows where a cross-validated model is confidently wrong are prime suspects (confident learning); review those first.</li><li><b>Be robust:</b> prefer bagging/random forests over AdaBoost-style methods that keep up-weighting the “hard” (mislabelled) points; use stronger regularisation, early stopping, label smoothing or robust losses.</li><li><b>Protect evaluation:</b> clean the validation/test labels carefully — noisy test labels cap and distort every metric.</li><li><b>Fix the source:</b> clearer labelling guidelines, multiple annotators with adjudication.</li></ul><p>Random noise mostly costs accuracy; systematic noise teaches the model the wrong rule and is far more harmful.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b2fca8e", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "24. A categorical feature has 50,000 distinct values. How do you use it?", "a": "<p>One-hot is impractical (huge, sparse, most levels with a handful of rows). Options: frequency encoding; target (mean) encoding with smoothing and out-of-fold computation so a row's own label never enters its feature; hashing into a fixed number of buckets; grouping rare levels into “Other”; learned embeddings in a neural model; or native handling in CatBoost/LightGBM. Also consider replacing the ID with <i>attributes</i> of the entity (merchant category, age, volume), which generalise to unseen values. Plan for categories never seen in training, and check that the model isn't just memorising IDs — validate on unseen entities if that's how it will be used.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b4560aa", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "25. In production you receive a category value the model never saw in training. What happens, and how should you design for it?", "a": "<p>Depending on the encoder: an exception (the service fails), an all-zeros one-hot row (treated like the baseline category), or an arbitrary code — all bad if unplanned. Design: <code>OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\")</code> or an explicit “unknown/rare” bucket that the model has seen during training (map infrequent training levels to it so it has learned a sensible effect); target encoders fall back to the global mean; hashing never fails. Log the rate of unknown values as a data-quality metric — a spike means an upstream change or drift and may signal that retraining is due.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b13f6a4", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "26. Your dataset has duplicate rows. Does it matter?", "a": "<p>Yes, in two ways. If duplicates (or near-duplicates such as the same customer on different days) land on both sides of the train/test split, the model is partly tested on data it memorised and the score is inflated. And within training, duplicates silently up-weight those examples and make standard errors look smaller than they are. First find out <i>why</i> they exist: a join that fanned out, repeated logging, or genuine repeated events. Remove true duplicates before splitting; for repeated entities use group-aware splits; if repetition reflects real frequency, keep it but make sure the split is by group.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-1f4c727", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "27. You're given data from two sources that measure the same thing differently. How do you combine them?", "a": "<p>Profile each source separately: units, definitions, time zones, coding of categories, missing-value conventions, collection period and population. Harmonise units and definitions explicitly; map categories to a shared scheme; keep a <code>source</code> column so the model can absorb remaining differences and so you can evaluate per source. Check the label means the same thing in both. Validate by training on one source and testing on the other — a large drop reveals systematic differences. If one source will be the only one in production, weight or fine-tune toward it, and make sure the evaluation set reflects that mix.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b16c32c", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "28. How do you find and deal with outliers before modelling?", "a": "<p>Look first (histograms, box plots, scatter plots), then use rules: IQR fences, robust z-scores based on median/MAD, or Isolation Forest for multivariate oddities. The key step is deciding what each outlier <i>is</i>: a data error (fix or remove), a rare but real value (keep — it may be the most valuable row), or the very thing you're trying to detect (fraud).</p><p>Treatment depends on the model: linear models and K-means are sensitive, so cap/winsorise, log-transform or use robust losses (Huber); tree models are largely insensitive to outliers in features but not in a regression target. Whatever thresholds you learn must come from training data only.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-2d1683f", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "29. The numeric target is heavily right-skewed (most values small, a few huge). How does that change your approach?", "a": "<p>Squared-error training will be dominated by the few huge values. Options: model log(1 + y) and transform back (remember the back-transformed mean is biased low — correct it or accept you're predicting something closer to the median); use a loss suited to the data (Poisson, Gamma or Tweedie for counts/amounts; MAE or Huber for robustness; quantile loss if a percentile matters); cap extreme values if they're errors. Choose the metric to match the business: RMSE punishes big misses, MAE treats all rupees equally, RMSLE measures relative error. Evaluate separately on the tail, since that's often where the money is.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-6ceeab2", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 3, "q": "30. You have a huge pool of unlabelled data and budget to label only 1,000 examples. How do you choose them?", "a": "<p>Start with a random sample of a few hundred — you need an unbiased set for evaluation and a first model. Spend the rest with <b>active learning</b>: train, then label the examples the model is least certain about (probabilities near the threshold, or disagreement among an ensemble), mixed with diversity sampling (pick from different clusters) so you don't label 500 near-identical edge cases. Repeat in rounds. Stretch the labels further with pretrained embeddings/transfer learning, weak supervision (heuristic labelling rules) and semi-supervised pseudo-labelling. Keep the evaluation set random and untouched by the selection process, or your metrics will be biased.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-6bdfb52", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 1, "q": "31. Your fraud model has 99.5% accuracy but the business says it's useless. Explain.", "a": "<p>If only 0.5% of transactions are fraud, predicting “not fraud” for everything scores 99.5% accuracy while catching nothing. Accuracy is dominated by the majority class. Look at the confusion matrix and at recall (share of fraud caught), precision (share of alerts that are real), PR-AUC, and the money: fraud value prevented versus cost of false alarms. Then fix the model's behaviour: class weights or resampling in training, and — most importantly — a decision threshold chosen for the business trade-off or the review team's capacity rather than the default 0.5.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-f3f428f", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "32. Precision is high but recall is very low. How do you raise recall, and what will it cost?", "a": "<p>The quickest lever is the threshold: lower it and recall rises while precision falls — plot the precision–recall curve and pick the point the business can live with. To move the whole curve outward rather than slide along it: class weights or resampling so the model pays more attention to positives; features that describe the missed positives (do error analysis on the false negatives — are they a distinct sub-type?); more positive examples; a stronger model. The cost is more false positives, so quantify it: “recall goes from 40% to 70%, alerts per day triple, precision drops from 90% to 55%”.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b585897", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "33. ROC-AUC is 0.95, yet at any usable threshold precision is under 10%. How can both be true?", "a": "<p>ROC-AUC measures ranking using the false-positive <i>rate</i>, whose denominator is all negatives. With 1 positive per 1,000 rows, a 1% false-positive rate still means about 10 false alarms for every real positive — precision around 9% even with excellent ranking. Nothing is broken; the metric just hides the base rate. Report PR-AUC and precision at the operating recall (or precision@k) instead, compare against the base rate, and judge the model on whether the top-scored cases are worth acting on.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-a57ff91", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "34. How do you choose the classification threshold for a model that will go live?", "a": "<p>From costs or constraints, not by default. If you can price the errors, and probabilities are calibrated, predict positive when p &gt; C_FP / (C_FP + C_FN). If there's a capacity limit (the team can review 200 cases a day), take the top 200 scores — that fixes the threshold. If there's a requirement (“recall must be ≥ 90%”), take the threshold that meets it with the best precision. Otherwise maximise F1 or Fβ. Choose it on a validation set, confirm on a separate test set, and revisit it whenever the class balance or the model changes — a threshold is tied to a specific score distribution.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-335fafb", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "35. You applied SMOTE and cross-validation scores jumped, but production performance is poor. What went wrong?", "a": "<p>Almost certainly SMOTE was applied <b>before</b> the split. Synthetic points are interpolations of real minority points, so validation folds contained near-copies of training rows — leakage — and the validation set no longer had the real class balance. Resampling must happen inside each training fold only (an imbalanced-learn <code>Pipeline</code>), and validation/test must keep the natural distribution. Also remember that after resampling the predicted probabilities are inflated, so thresholds chosen on balanced data don't transfer; recalibrate or tune the threshold on untouched data. Often class weights plus threshold tuning do as well with less risk.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-f84f4c2", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "36. After you used class weights, the model's predicted probabilities look far too high. Why, and does it matter?", "a": "<p>Weighting (or over/under-sampling) tells the model positives are more common than they are, so its scores are no longer estimates of the true probability — a “0.6” may correspond to a real rate of 5%. It doesn't matter if you only need ranking or a tuned threshold. It matters a lot if the number is used as a probability: expected-loss calculations, pricing, risk reports. Fix by recalibrating on a held-out set with the natural class balance (Platt/isotonic), or correct the prior analytically, or train without weights and handle imbalance through the threshold alone.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-fedc7cc", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "37. For a cancer-screening model, which metric do you optimise? And for a spam filter?", "a": "<p><b>Screening:</b> a missed cancer (false negative) is far worse than an unnecessary follow-up test, so prioritise recall/sensitivity — e.g. maximise specificity subject to sensitivity ≥ 95–99% — because positives go on to a confirmatory test. <b>Spam:</b> sending a real email to spam (false positive) is worse than letting one spam through, so prioritise precision, with recall as the secondary goal. In both cases I'd say the metric follows from the asymmetric cost of errors and from what happens next in the workflow, and I'd report the full confusion matrix at the chosen operating point.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-4d8589e", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "38. Model A has higher AUC, model B has higher F1. Which do you ship?", "a": "<p>They answer different questions: AUC measures ranking over all thresholds; F1 measures one operating point (and depends on the threshold each model happened to use). So first compare fairly — tune each model's threshold on validation data for the metric that reflects the real decision. If the product uses a fixed decision (alert / don't alert), compare precision and recall at the operating point that matters. If it uses the ranking (top-k recommendations, prioritised queues), AUC or precision@k matters more. Then check stability across folds, calibration, latency and cost. Small differences inside the noise → ship the simpler one.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b71b100", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "39. Your regression model has R² = 0.9 but the users say the predictions are useless. Why might that be?", "a": "<ul><li>R² is relative to predicting the mean; if the target varies hugely, 0.9 can still leave errors too large for the decision. Report MAE in real units.</li><li>The users care about a range where the model is weak (small values, the tail, a specific segment).</li><li>R² was measured in-sample or with leakage (e.g. a lagged target that isn't available at prediction time).</li><li>The model is biased in a systematic way — good correlation, wrong level.</li><li>Predictions arrive too late or aren't actionable.</li></ul><p>I'd ask what accuracy they need, in their units, for which decisions, and evaluate against that.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-cc83279", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 3, "q": "40. The test set has only 200 rows. How confident are you in a reported accuracy of 85%?", "a": "<p>Not very. The standard error is √(0.85 × 0.15 / 200) ≈ 2.5 points, so a 95% interval is roughly 80–90%. A “2-point improvement” between models on this set is noise. With a rare positive class it's worse — recall might rest on a dozen examples. What I'd do: report confidence intervals (bootstrap the test set for metrics like AUC or F1); use repeated or nested cross-validation to use all the data; compare models with paired tests on the same examples (McNemar); and collect a larger test set if the decision is important.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-07d9f51", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "41. Two features are 0.97 correlated. Do you remove one?", "a": "<p>It depends on the goal. For <b>prediction</b> with trees or regularised models, it rarely hurts accuracy; keeping both is fine. For <b>interpretation</b> it's a problem: in a linear model the coefficients become unstable with large standard errors and can flip sign, and tree importances/SHAP split credit arbitrarily between the two. Then I'd drop one (keep the cheaper, more reliable or more interpretable), combine them (average, ratio, PCA component), or use Ridge. Check with VIF, and confirm by cross-validation that removing one doesn't cost accuracy. Fewer redundant features also means less to maintain and monitor.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-f97edde", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "42. You have 5,000 candidate features. How do you narrow them down?", "a": "<ol><li><b>Cheap filters:</b> drop constants, near-constants, duplicates, columns that are mostly missing, and anything unavailable at prediction time or leaky.</li><li><b>Redundancy:</b> cluster highly correlated features and keep one per cluster.</li><li><b>Relevance:</b> univariate screens (mutual information) as a rough cut.</li><li><b>Model-based:</b> L1-regularised model or gradient boosting, then permutation importance or SHAP on validation data; recursive elimination if affordable.</li><li><b>Stability:</b> keep features selected consistently across folds/bootstraps.</li></ol><p>All selection happens inside cross-validation. I'd stop when dropping more starts to cost validation performance, and favour features that are cheap, stable and explainable.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-3bde8de", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "43. Feature importance ranks a random-ID column near the top. What does that mean?", "a": "<p>Either the importance method is misleading or the ID leaks. Impurity-based importance from trees favours high-cardinality columns: an ID offers thousands of split points, so by chance some reduce impurity on the training data. Permutation importance on a held-out set will show its real value (near zero). If it's still important on held-out data, the ID carries information — e.g. IDs assigned in time order, or in blocks by region or by outcome — which is leakage or a proxy. Either way remove it, and prefer permutation importance or SHAP on validation data for decisions.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-f6fa955", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "44. A simple logistic regression scores 0.84 AUC and a tuned XGBoost scores 0.85. Which do you deploy?", "a": "<p>Probably the logistic regression. A 0.01 gap is often inside cross-validation noise (check fold-by-fold results), and the linear model is cheaper to serve, faster, easier to explain to regulators and stakeholders, easier to debug and monitor, and more stable under drift. I'd deploy XGBoost only if the gain is statistically solid <i>and</i> worth real money at the operating point, and the latency/maintenance budget allows. The small gap also tells me the remaining signal isn't in non-linearities — better features or data are likely the next lever for either model.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-23126d9", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "45. The regulator requires every loan decision to be explainable. How does that change your modelling?", "a": "<p>I'd start from inherently interpretable models: logistic regression on sensibly binned features (a scorecard), a GAM, or a shallow tree; if a boosted model is needed, add monotonic constraints (risk can't fall as debt rises) and limit interactions. Each decision needs reason codes — the top factors that pushed the score down — from coefficients or SHAP. I'd exclude protected attributes, test for proxies and measure outcomes across groups, document data, assumptions and limitations in a model card, check stability over time, and keep full versioning for audits. Accuracy is traded for transparency deliberately, and I'd quantify what that trade costs.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-4167a34", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "46. Predictions must be returned in under 10 ms. What do you change?", "a": "<p>Work out where the time goes — usually feature retrieval, not the model. Then: precompute features and store them in a low-latency key-value store; drop features that need slow lookups; choose a light model (linear, small gradient-boosted model with limited trees/depth) or distil a large model into a small one; compile/export to an optimised runtime (ONNX Runtime, Treelite); keep the model in memory, avoid Python overhead on the hot path; cache results for repeated requests; use a two-stage design (cheap filter, expensive model only for the uncertain few). Set a timeout with a sensible fallback, and measure p99 latency, not the average.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-4e7086b", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "47. When would you choose a linear model over gradient boosting, and when the reverse?", "a": "<p><b>Linear:</b> small data; very high-dimensional sparse features (text); need for clear explanation and calibrated probabilities; strict latency; the relationship is roughly additive; you need to extrapolate beyond the training range; you want a stable baseline.</p><p><b>Boosting:</b> medium/large tabular data with non-linear effects, thresholds and interactions; mixed feature types and missing values; accuracy is the priority and you have time to tune and monitor.</p><p>In practice I build the linear baseline first; the size of the gap between it and boosting tells me how much non-linearity matters and whether the extra complexity is justified.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-d986b98", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "48. Your manager asks, “Why not just use deep learning?” for a tabular churn problem. What do you answer?", "a": "<p>For tabular data of ordinary size, gradient-boosted trees usually match or beat neural networks, with far less tuning, training cost and data. Tabular features are a mix of scales and types with sharp thresholds and many irrelevant columns — the kind of structure trees handle naturally and networks don't. Deep learning earns its place with images, text, audio, sequences, very large datasets, or when you need learned embeddings for huge-cardinality IDs or to combine modalities. I'd offer to benchmark both against a baseline, but set expectations: the gains here are more likely from better features and labels than from a bigger model.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-7077ce6", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "49. A rule-based system is already in production. How do you decide whether ML is worth it?", "a": "<p>Treat the rules as the baseline. Measure them on a labelled sample: precision, recall, cost of errors, and how much effort they take to maintain. Estimate the upside: how much better could a model plausibly be (a quick prototype on historical data), and what is one point of improvement worth? Weigh that against the costs ML adds — labels, pipelines, monitoring, retraining, explainability. ML is worth it when patterns are too complex or fast-changing for rules, there's enough labelled data, and errors are tolerable. Often the best design is hybrid: rules as guardrails and features, the model for ranking, and an A/B test to prove the gain.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-3671775", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "50. A stakeholder wants one more feature added the day before launch. What do you check?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Availability at prediction time:</b> does it exist, at that moment, in production, with the same definition as in training?</li><li><b>Leakage:</b> is it recorded after the outcome?</li><li><b>Quality and coverage:</b> missing rate, history length, stability over time, who owns the pipeline.</li><li><b>Value:</b> does it improve cross-validated performance beyond noise, especially on the slices that matter?</li><li><b>Risk:</b> fairness/legal issues, extra latency, a new dependency to monitor.</li></ul><p>A day before launch I'd almost always ship without it and add it in the next iteration behind an A/B test — a late, unvalidated feature is a classic source of incidents.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b453d4e", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "51. In your linear regression, a coefficient has the opposite sign to what everyone expects. What's going on?", "a": "<p>Most often multicollinearity: the feature is strongly correlated with another one, and the two coefficients are jointly unstable — one goes up, the other goes down to compensate. Check VIFs and the correlation matrix; refit without the partner feature and see whether the sign flips back. Other causes: an omitted confounder (Simpson's paradox — the sign is right “holding other things fixed” but wrong marginally); the coefficient isn't statistically different from zero (look at its confidence interval); outliers or high-leverage points; a data/encoding error; or the expectation is simply wrong. A coefficient always means “effect with the other features held constant”, which is not the same as the raw relationship.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-ae13eed", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "52. Your residual plot shows a funnel shape (errors grow with the prediction). What does it mean and what do you do?", "a": "<p>Heteroscedasticity: the error variance isn't constant. Coefficients are still unbiased, but standard errors, p-values and confidence/prediction intervals are wrong, and the fit pays too much attention to the high-variance region. Confirm with a Breusch–Pagan or White test. Fixes: transform the target (log or Box–Cox — common for prices, incomes, counts); weighted least squares with weights ∝ 1/variance; heteroscedasticity-robust (HC3) standard errors if you only need valid inference; or model the right distribution directly (Poisson/Gamma GLM). If you need prediction intervals, use quantile regression so the interval width can vary.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-13a0c4e", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "53. Logistic regression prints a convergence warning and the coefficients are enormous. Why?", "a": "<p>Probably (quasi-)perfect separation: some feature or combination predicts the class perfectly, so the likelihood keeps improving as the weights grow without limit and the optimiser never converges. That feature may be leakage, or a rare category that appears in only one class. Other causes: unscaled features making the optimisation ill-conditioned; too few iterations; strong collinearity. Fixes: standardise the inputs; keep (or increase) L2 regularisation — lower C; raise <code>max_iter</code>; remove or investigate the separating feature; merge rare categories. If you need unbiased inference with separation, use Firth's penalised likelihood.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-dbb401a", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "54. Your random forest is very accurate but too slow and too large to deploy. What are your options?", "a": "<p>Size and latency grow with number of trees × depth. Options: fewer trees (accuracy usually plateaus early — check the OOB curve); limit depth or raise <code>min_samples_leaf</code> so trees are smaller; cut features; train on a subsample (<code>max_samples</code>). Or switch models: gradient boosting typically reaches the same accuracy with far fewer, shallower trees; distil the forest into a small boosted model or linear model. Engineering: compile trees (Treelite/ONNX), use float32, batch predictions, or precompute scores in batch if real-time isn't truly required. Measure the accuracy–latency trade-off at each step.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-a6540d1", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "55. Your gradient-boosting model: training loss keeps falling, validation loss starts rising after 300 trees. What do you do?", "a": "<p>That's overfitting in the number of rounds — each extra tree is now fitting noise. Use <b>early stopping</b>: keep the model at the best validation iteration (≈300). Then improve the trade-off: lower the learning rate and let early stopping find the new tree count; make trees weaker (smaller <code>max_depth</code>/<code>num_leaves</code>, larger <code>min_child_weight</code>/<code>min_child_samples</code>); add row and column subsampling; raise L2/γ. Make sure the early-stopping set is separate from the final test set, and when retraining on all data use the tree count found (scaled up slightly for the larger dataset).</p>"}, {"id": "situational-752f1a3", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "56. XGBoost with default settings performs worse than a random forest on your data. Why could that be?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Small or noisy dataset:</b> boosting chases noise; averaging (RF) is more robust.</li><li><b>Not tuned:</b> boosting is much more sensitive — the default 100 trees at learning rate 0.3 with depth 6 may over- or underfit. Tune learning rate with early stopping, depth, min_child_weight, subsampling, regularisation.</li><li><b>No early stopping</b> or an unsuitable evaluation metric.</li><li><b>Label noise or outliers</b> in the target.</li><li><b>Categoricals encoded poorly</b> (arbitrary integer codes).</li></ul><p>A random forest is hard to get badly wrong; boosting usually wins only after tuning. If the tuned gap stays negative, accept the forest.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-0883b69", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "57. A kNN classifier worked well in your prototype with 5 features and collapsed when you added 200 more. Why?", "a": "<p>The curse of dimensionality. In high dimensions distances concentrate — the nearest and farthest neighbours are almost equally far — so “nearest” stops meaning “similar”. Worse, most of the 200 new features are probably irrelevant, and every one contributes equally to the distance, drowning the five that matter. Also check they were scaled. Fixes: feature selection; dimensionality reduction (PCA or learned embeddings) before kNN; a metric that weights features (metric learning); or switch to a model that ignores irrelevant features by itself, such as gradient boosting or a regularised linear model.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-ea48afe", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "58. Your SVM takes hours to train on 2 million rows. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Kernel SVM training scales between quadratic and cubic in the number of rows, so it's the wrong tool at this size. Options: a linear SVM solver (<code>LinearSVC</code> or <code>SGDClassifier(loss=\"hinge\")</code>), which scales linearly; if non-linearity is needed, approximate the kernel with random Fourier features or Nyström and train a linear model on top; train on a well-chosen subsample; or change model family — gradient boosting for tabular data. Also confirm features are scaled (unscaled data slows the solver dramatically) and that C and γ aren't at extremes producing a huge number of support vectors.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-2ddcb69", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "59. K-means gives different clusters every time you run it. Is that a problem?", "a": "<p>K-means only finds a local optimum that depends on the starting centroids, so some variation is expected. Reduce it with k-means++ initialisation, many restarts (<code>n_init</code>) keeping the lowest inertia, and a fixed seed for reproducibility. But large instability is a message: the data may not have K well-separated, roughly spherical clusters. Check by measuring agreement between runs (adjusted Rand index) and silhouette scores; try other values of K; scale features and remove outliers; consider a model that fits the shape better (GMM, HDBSCAN). If no K gives stable clusters, say so — the structure may be a continuum rather than groups.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-31b1480", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "60. Naive Bayes outputs probabilities that are almost always 0.00 or 1.00. Should you trust them?", "a": "<p>No. Naive Bayes multiplies the evidence from each feature as if they were independent. Real features are correlated (in text, “credit” and “card” travel together), so the same evidence is counted several times and the posterior is pushed to the extremes. The ranking and the predicted class are often still good; the probability values are not. If you need real probabilities, calibrate on held-out data (isotonic or sigmoid via <code>CalibratedClassifierCV</code>) or use logistic regression. Also check smoothing: with α too small, a rare word can swing the result on its own.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-fa49b7e", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "61. Your dataset has many rows per customer. How should you split it, and what happens if you split randomly?", "a": "<p>Split by customer (GroupKFold / a group-wise holdout) so all rows from one customer are on the same side. With a random split, the model sees other rows from the same customer in training and can recognise the customer rather than learn general patterns; the validation score then measures memorisation and overstates performance on new customers — sometimes dramatically. The rule: the split should mimic how the model will be used. If it will score new customers, hold out customers; if it will score existing customers in future months, hold out by time; often you need both.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-ace5e5d", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "62. You tried 200 hyperparameter combinations and report the best cross-validation score. Is that number trustworthy?", "a": "<p>It's optimistically biased: the maximum of 200 noisy estimates is partly luck (the “winner's curse”), more so with small data. The honest performance estimate comes from data not used in the selection: a final untouched test set, or nested cross-validation where the search is repeated inside each outer fold. Also look at whether the top configurations are within one standard error of each other — if so, prefer the simplest. And don't go back and re-tune after looking at the test set, or it stops being a test set.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-1dccb03", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "63. Offline, the new model beats the old one by 3%. In the A/B test there's no difference. Give possible reasons.", "a": "<ul><li>The offline metric isn't the business metric (better AUC ≠ more conversions).</li><li>Offline evaluation was optimistic: leakage, a split that doesn't mirror production, old data.</li><li>Training–serving skew: features differ online.</li><li>Offline data were generated under the old model's decisions (selection/feedback bias), so they can't show what the new model would do.</li><li>The improvement is in cases that don't change any decision.</li><li>The new model is slower and the latency cancels the gain.</li><li>The test is underpowered or too short; novelty effects.</li></ul><p>I'd check implementation parity first (same inputs → same outputs offline and online), then power, then whether the offline metric correlates with the online one historically.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-5ee23fd", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "64. The A/B test shows p = 0.04 after three days and the PM wants to ship. What do you say?", "a": "<p>Not yet, if the planned duration was longer. Checking repeatedly and stopping the first time p &lt; 0.05 inflates the false-positive rate well beyond 5%. Three days also misses weekly seasonality and may be a novelty effect. I'd ask: was the sample size fixed in advance and has it been reached? Is the split healthy (no sample-ratio mismatch)? What do the confidence interval and guardrail metrics say? If we need early decisions, use a sequential testing method designed for it. If the planned sample is reached and the effect is practically meaningful with guardrails intact, ship.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b7e0000", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "65. An A/B test result is “not statistically significant”. Does that mean the change has no effect?", "a": "<p>No — it means the data are consistent with no effect, not that they prove it. Look at the confidence interval: if it's narrow around zero (say −0.2% to +0.3%), you can conclude any effect is too small to matter. If it's wide (−2% to +6%), the test was underpowered and is inconclusive; a worthwhile effect may well exist. Check the power calculation: was the sample large enough for the minimum effect we care about? Options: run longer, reduce variance (CUPED, better metric), or make a bolder change. Don't slice into segments until something is “significant” — that's p-hacking.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-13010dd", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "66. You can't run an A/B test (legal or practical reasons). How do you estimate the impact of the model?", "a": "<p>Use quasi-experimental methods, each with assumptions to defend: <b>difference-in-differences</b> (compare the change in a treated group with a similar untreated group; needs parallel trends); <b>staggered or geographic roll-out</b> used as a natural experiment; <b>synthetic control</b> (build a weighted mix of untreated units that matched the treated one before launch); <b>regression discontinuity</b> if treatment depends on a score threshold; <b>propensity matching/weighting</b> to balance observed confounders; <b>interrupted time series</b> with a forecast of what would have happened. I'd run placebo checks, state the assumptions plainly, and present the result as a range, not a point.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-64bf882", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "67. You're asked to validate a forecasting model. A colleague used 5-fold cross-validation with shuffling. What's wrong?", "a": "<p>Shuffling puts future observations in the training folds and past ones in validation, so the model “predicts” days whose neighbours it has already seen — and with lag features, it may literally have the answer. The score is far too optimistic. Use rolling-origin evaluation: train on data up to time t, forecast the next horizon, move forward, repeat. Match the horizon and retraining frequency to production, leave a gap equal to any feature look-ahead or label delay, compare with naive and seasonal-naive baselines, and report error by horizon.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-80dfe95", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "68. The treatment group in your experiment has 50.9% of users instead of the planned 50%. Do you care?", "a": "<p>With large samples, yes. Run a chi-square test on the counts: at a million users, 50.9/49.1 is wildly unlikely by chance — a <b>sample ratio mismatch</b>. It means assignment or logging is broken in a way that probably differs between groups (redirects losing users, bots filtered unequally, a crash in one variant, a tracking bug), so the two groups are no longer comparable and the results can't be trusted, whatever the p-value on the main metric. Stop interpreting, find the cause (break down by browser, platform, date), fix it and rerun.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-b7d8783", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 3, "q": "69. You tested 20 metrics and one is significant at p = 0.03. How do you report it?", "a": "<p>With 20 independent tests at α = 0.05 you expect one false positive by chance, and the probability of at least one is about 64%. So a single p = 0.03 among 20 is weak evidence. Ideally a primary metric was declared in advance; everything else is exploratory. Apply a correction — Bonferroni (0.05/20 = 0.0025) to control the chance of any false positive, or Benjamini–Hochberg to control the false-discovery rate. I'd report it as a hypothesis worth a follow-up test, not as a finding, and show all 20 results rather than only the “winner”.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-4a9a6c6", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "70. How do you make sure your offline evaluation will predict real-world performance?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Split like production:</b> hold out the most recent period and unseen entities.</li><li><b>Features as of prediction time:</b> point-in-time correct joins, no late-arriving fields.</li><li><b>Same pipeline</b> for training, evaluation and serving.</li><li><b>Metric tied to the decision:</b> the operating threshold or top-k that will be used, weighted by business value.</li><li><b>Representative population:</b> include the cases the current system rejects, or acknowledge the selection bias.</li><li><b>Slices and uncertainty:</b> report segments and confidence intervals.</li><li><b>Close the loop:</b> shadow deployment, then compare offline predictions with online outcomes and track how well offline gains have translated historically.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "situational-7e807c7", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "71. Model accuracy dropped ten points overnight. What's your investigation plan?", "a": "<p>A sudden drop is almost always a pipeline problem, not the world changing overnight.</p><ol><li><b>Is the alert real?</b> Metric code, label delay, sample size.</li><li><b>What changed yesterday?</b> Deployments (model, feature code, upstream services), schema changes, config.</li><li><b>Data quality:</b> null rates, new categories, unit or encoding changes, a join returning fewer rows, a stale feature table, default values being served.</li><li><b>Drift per feature</b> and the prediction distribution — which inputs moved?</li><li><b>Which slice?</b> If one region/platform accounts for the drop, the cause is local.</li></ol><p>Mitigate first (roll back or switch to the fallback), fix the root cause, then add the missing test or monitor. I wouldn't retrain until I knew why — retraining on broken data bakes the bug in.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-1110e98", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "72. Performance has been sliding slowly over six months. How is that different from a sudden drop?", "a": "<p>Gradual decay points to drift: the population of inputs is shifting (data drift) or the relationship between inputs and outcome is changing (concept drift) — customer behaviour, competitors, prices, fraud tactics. Confirm by comparing feature distributions and performance by month, and by backtesting how accuracy falls with model age. The response is a retraining policy rather than a hotfix: retrain on a recent window, at a cadence matched to the measured decay, with validation gates; consider recency weighting; add features that are more stable; and set alerts on drift and performance so the next slide is caught in weeks, not months.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-65fe254", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "73. The model was excellent in the notebook but is poor once deployed. What are the usual reasons?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Training–serving skew:</b> features computed by different code or from different sources online; different default for missing values; different time zone or units.</li><li><b>Leakage in training:</b> features that used information not available at prediction time.</li><li><b>Stale or late features</b> online (yesterday's aggregate instead of real-time).</li><li><b>Population mismatch:</b> trained on a filtered or historical population.</li><li><b>Preprocessing not shipped</b> with the model (scaler, encoder, vocabulary versions).</li><li><b>Library/version differences.</b></li></ul><p>Diagnose by logging production feature vectors and predictions, replaying them through the offline pipeline, and diffing row by row. Prevent with a single feature definition (feature store), the preprocessing packaged inside the model artefact, and a shadow deployment before launch.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-99e277b", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "74. True labels arrive two months after the prediction. How do you monitor the model meanwhile?", "a": "<p>Use leading indicators: data-quality checks (schema, nulls, ranges, volumes); drift in input features (PSI, KS) against a reference window; drift in the distribution of predictions and of the share above threshold; proxy outcomes available sooner (early delinquency instead of default, clicks instead of purchases); and human review of a small random sample to get quick labels. Performance can also be estimated from calibrated confidence. When labels do arrive, join them back, compute the real metrics and check how well the early signals predicted them — that tells you which alerts deserve trust.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-9ddaa38", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "75. Your drift monitor fires an alert on three features. Do you retrain?", "a": "<p>Not automatically. First check it's real and not a data bug (a unit change looks like drift, and retraining on it would be wrong). Then ask whether it matters: are these features important to the model? Has the prediction distribution or any performance proxy moved? With very large samples, statistical tests flag trivial shifts, so judge by effect size. If inputs shifted in an important way and performance is at risk, retrain on recent data and validate against the current model on the newest labelled period before promoting. If it's seasonal and expected, widen the reference window instead of retraining every time.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-9a73b14", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "76. You retrained the model on fresh data and it's worse than the one in production. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Don't promote it — that's exactly what a validation gate is for. Then find out why: the new data may contain a pipeline bug, label errors or labels that aren't mature yet (recent rows with incomplete outcomes); the training window may now exclude a useful period or include an abnormal one (a promotion, an outage); hyperparameters tuned for the old data may not fit; the evaluation set may favour the old model (it was tuned on it); or it's just run-to-run variance. Compare both models on the same recent holdout with confidence intervals and by slice, and keep the champion until a challenger clearly wins.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-faaaac5", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "77. An upstream team changed a field from rupees to thousands of rupees without telling you. How would you have caught it?", "a": "<p>With data validation at the boundary: schema and range checks on every batch or request (expected type, min/max, mean within tolerance of the reference), and distribution monitoring — a 1000× shift in the mean lights up PSI immediately. Plus data contracts with upstream owners so changes are announced and versioned, alerts on the prediction distribution (the model's outputs would shift too), and a canary/shadow stage for upstream changes. In the service itself, fail safe: when an input is out of range, flag it and fall back rather than produce a confident nonsense prediction.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-d475e5e", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "78. How do you safely replace a model that millions of users depend on?", "a": "<ol><li><b>Offline gates:</b> beats the current model on a recent holdout and on key slices; passes latency, size, fairness and calibration checks.</li><li><b>Shadow mode:</b> runs on live traffic, predictions logged but not used; compare with the champion and check for skew and latency.</li><li><b>Canary:</b> 1–5% of traffic with automatic rollback on guardrail breaches.</li><li><b>A/B test</b> for business impact at sufficient power.</li><li><b>Gradual ramp</b> to 100%, keeping the old model warm for instant rollback.</li></ol><p>Throughout: version model + data + code together, and monitor service health, inputs, outputs and outcomes.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-08c3c0d", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "79. Latency of your model service doubled after a new version was deployed. How do you investigate?", "a": "<p>Compare the two versions stage by stage: feature fetching, preprocessing, inference, post-processing, network. Candidates: a bigger model (more trees, deeper, more features); a new feature that needs a slow lookup or an extra service call; preprocessing moved into Python loops; lost batching or caching; a different runtime, thread settings or hardware; cold starts or memory pressure causing swapping; larger payloads. Check p50 vs p99 — if only the tail grew, look at a dependency or garbage collection. Roll back if the SLO is breached, reproduce with a load test, profile, and add a latency budget check to the release pipeline.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-3670665", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 3, "q": "80. Your recommender's own suggestions determine what users click, and those clicks become training data. What's the risk and the fix?", "a": "<p>A feedback loop. The model only gets feedback on items it chose to show, so popular items collect more clicks, are recommended more, and the rest never get a chance; the model's view of the world narrows and offline metrics computed on its own logs look better than reality. Fixes: reserve a small share of traffic for exploration (random or bandit-based) to get unbiased data; log the probability with which each item was shown and use inverse-propensity weighting in training and evaluation; correct for position bias; monitor diversity, coverage and new-item exposure; and evaluate with online experiments, not only logged data.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-d9afec6", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "81. A loan model can only observe repayment for applicants who were approved. Why is that a problem?", "a": "<p>Selection bias: the training data contain no outcomes for the people the previous policy rejected, so the model learns “who defaults among those we already liked” and can be badly wrong for the rejected region — exactly where a new policy might want to expand. Remedies: reject inference techniques (with caution — they rely on assumptions); approve a small random sample of would-be rejects to obtain unbiased labels (the gold standard, at a controlled cost); use bureau or external outcome data for rejected applicants; and at least evaluate and monitor separately near the old decision boundary.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-8aea60c", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "82. How would you decide how often to retrain a model?", "a": "<p>Measure the decay instead of guessing: backtest by training at several past dates and scoring each model on later months — the curve of performance against model age shows how fast it goes stale. Combine with the cost of staleness (lost revenue per point of accuracy) and the cost of retraining (compute, validation, risk). Fast-moving domains (fraud, ads, recommendations) need daily or weekly retraining; slow ones (credit risk) quarterly. Add triggers on drift or performance alerts for the unexpected, respect label delay when choosing the training window, and automate the pipeline with validation gates so frequency is cheap and safe.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-1c874c1", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "83. Your demand forecast is consistently too low during festival weeks. How do you fix it?", "a": "<p>The model doesn't know the festival is coming. Add calendar and event features: holiday flags, days until/since the festival, festival-specific effects, and — because festivals such as Diwali move in the calendar — align by event date rather than by week number. Include promotion and price features. Make sure the training window contains several past occurrences, and weight or model them explicitly since they're rare. If the model is tree-based, remember it can't predict levels above anything seen; forecast uplift relative to a baseline instead. Also check whether past sales were capped by stock-outs — the history then understates true demand.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-6715826", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "84. You need to forecast sales for a product that launched last week. There's no history. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Cold start: borrow strength from elsewhere. Use the launch curves of similar past products (same category, price band, brand, season) as a prior — average them or find nearest neighbours on product attributes. A global model trained across all products with attribute features (not just each product's own lags) can predict for a new one. Add signals available before or just after launch: pre-orders, page views, search volume, marketing spend. Give wide prediction intervals, update quickly as each day's sales arrive (Bayesian or simply re-forecasting daily), and tell planners how uncertain it is.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-5bd91e8", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "85. Your gradient-boosting forecast flat-lines at the top for a fast-growing product. Why?", "a": "<p>Tree models predict averages of training targets within leaves, so they can never output a value larger than what they saw: with a rising trend, future values lie outside the training range and the forecast saturates. Fixes: remove the trend first and model what's left — predict the change (difference) or growth rate, or the ratio to a recent rolling mean, then convert back; or fit a simple linear/exponential trend and let the trees model the residuals; or use a model that extrapolates (linear, ETS, ARIMA, Prophet) for the trend component. Validate specifically on the most recent, highest-level period.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-1e58532", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "86. A sentiment classifier trained on product reviews performs badly on tweets. Why, and what are your options?", "a": "<p>Domain shift: tweets are short, informal, full of slang, emojis, hashtags and sarcasm; the vocabulary and the way sentiment is expressed differ from long, structured reviews, so the features the model relies on are missing or mean something else. Options, cheapest first: label a few hundred tweets and evaluate properly; fine-tune the model on in-domain labelled tweets (even a small set helps a lot with a pretrained language model); continue pretraining on unlabelled tweets; adapt preprocessing (emojis, elongated words, handles); combine both domains in training with a domain indicator. Then monitor — language on social media drifts quickly.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-220f43b", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "87. Your recommender keeps showing the same popular items to everyone. How do you fix that?", "a": "<p>Popularity bias: popular items have the most interactions, so the model learns them best and the feedback loop reinforces it. Remedies: normalise or down-weight popularity in training (e.g. sample negatives by popularity, inverse-propensity weighting); add personalised signals and content features so niche items can be matched; re-rank for diversity and novelty (limit items per category, maximal-marginal-relevance); reserve slots for exploration and new items; and change what you measure — add coverage, diversity and long-term engagement to click-through rate, since CTR alone rewards the safe popular choice. Check by user segment whether heavy and light users both get relevant variety.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-d620d2b", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "88. You must build a classifier but have only 50 positive examples. What's your plan?", "a": "<p>With 50 positives, variance is the enemy. Keep the model simple and regularised (logistic regression, small tree ensemble) with a handful of strong, domain-driven features; use repeated stratified cross-validation and report wide confidence intervals; never split positives so thinly that a fold has two or three. Get more signal: transfer learning or pretrained embeddings; weak labels from rules; search for more positives with the first model and have experts verify them (active learning); consider framing it as anomaly detection trained on the plentiful negatives. Be honest that the performance estimate is rough until more positives exist.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-4c159cc", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "89. You clustered customers into five segments. The marketing head asks, “Are these the right segments?” How do you answer?", "a": "<p>There's no ground truth, so “right” means stable, distinct and useful. I'd show: <b>stability</b> — do the same segments appear with different seeds, samples and months? <b>Separation</b> — silhouette scores and how different the segment profiles are on the key variables; <b>interpretability</b> — a plain description and name for each (“high-value lapsed”); <b>actionability</b> — would each get a different treatment, and is each big enough to matter? Then validate on behaviour not used in the clustering, and ultimately with a test: do targeted campaigns per segment beat a one-size-fits-all campaign? If not, the segmentation isn't right for this purpose.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-3851af2", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 3, "q": "90. Your anomaly detector produces 5,000 alerts a day and the operations team reviews 100. What do you change?", "a": "<p>The alert volume must match the capacity. Rank alerts by anomaly score and expected impact (score × amount at risk) and send only the top 100; set the threshold from capacity rather than a fixed contamination rate. Reduce false alarms: add context features (what's normal for <i>this</i> customer, hour, device); remove known benign patterns with rules; group alerts that belong to the same incident. Use the analysts' decisions as labels to train a supervised model on top of the anomaly score — precision usually jumps. Track precision at 100 and the estimated misses (sample below the threshold occasionally) so recall isn't forgotten.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-a026c6a", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "91. The business asks for a model with “95% accuracy”. How do you respond?", "a": "<p>I'd unpack the number before agreeing to it. What decision will the model drive, and what do the two kinds of error cost? Is accuracy even the right measure (with 3% positives, 97% is trivial)? What does the current process achieve — that's the real benchmark? What's the ceiling given label noise and the available data? Then I'd translate the request into a metric tied to value (e.g. “catch 80% of fraud with fewer than 1 false alarm per 50 transactions”), build a baseline quickly to learn what's feasible, and come back with a realistic target, a range of uncertainty and what it would take to do better.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-3ec7825", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "92. Your model disagrees with a senior domain expert about an important case. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Treat it as information, not a contest. Look at the case: which features drove the prediction (SHAP/local explanation)? Is the input data correct? Is the case unlike anything in training (out of distribution)? Does the expert know something the model can't see — context that isn't a feature? If the model is wrong, I've found a missing feature or a data gap. If the expert is using a rule of thumb the data contradict, I'd show similar past cases and their outcomes. For high-stakes decisions I'd keep the human in the loop, log disagreements, and review them regularly — they're the cheapest source of model improvement and of trust.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-c538bc8", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "93. You discover the model's error rate is much higher for one demographic group. What are your next steps?", "a": "<p>Measure properly first: per-group error rates, false-positive and false-negative rates, calibration and sample sizes with confidence intervals. Find the cause: under-representation in training data, poorer data quality for that group, label bias from historical decisions, or features that work as proxies. Mitigate: collect or re-weight data; fix features; consider fairness-aware training or group-aware thresholds where that's lawful and appropriate; add human review for affected cases. Escalate to product/legal/compliance — which fairness criterion to satisfy is a policy decision with trade-offs, not something I should settle alone. Then add the group metrics to routine monitoring and document everything.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-d9d09af", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "94. How do you explain to a non-technical executive why the model declined a particular customer?", "a": "<p>Start with the decision and the two or three main reasons in plain language: “Declined mainly because of three missed payments in the last six months and credit use above 90%; a long relationship with us counted in their favour.” These come from the model's per-prediction explanation (coefficients or SHAP), translated out of feature names. Add what would change the outcome (“with utilisation below 50%, the application would pass”), how reliable the model is for customers like this one, and the safeguard (appeal/human review). No algorithm names or statistics unless asked — and I'd be clear that these are the model's reasons, not proof of cause.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-f586d25", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "95. You have two weeks to deliver a churn model. What do you prioritise and what do you skip?", "a": "<p><b>Prioritise:</b> a precise definition of churn and of the prediction moment (days 1–2); a leak-free training set with point-in-time features; a time-based validation that mirrors use; a simple baseline, then one strong model (gradient boosting) with sensible defaults; a metric tied to the retention team's capacity (lift in the top decile); a short error analysis; and a hand-off the business can use — scored list plus top reasons per customer.</p><p><b>Skip for now:</b> exotic models, exhaustive tuning, stacking, real-time serving, elaborate feature stores. I'd state the limitations and a follow-up plan: A/B test of the retention campaign, monitoring, and uplift modelling later.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-0549981", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "96. Your model improves the target metric, but customer complaints go up. What happened?", "a": "<p>The metric being optimised is a proxy, and the model found a way to raise it that hurts something not measured — clickbait raises click-through, aggressive fraud blocking lowers fraud losses but declines good customers, a retention model pesters people. This is why launches need guardrail metrics (complaints, refunds, unsubscribe rate, latency, long-term retention) alongside the primary metric. I'd quantify the trade-off, look at which users and which predictions drive the complaints, and then change the objective or add constraints: include the cost of false positives in the threshold, cap contact frequency, or optimise a longer-term outcome.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-ea0eeb5", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "97. A stakeholder asks you to just “use AI” on a problem. How do you decide whether ML is appropriate at all?", "a": "<p>I'd check five things. Is there a clear decision that a prediction would improve, and is the value worth the effort? Is there a learnable pattern and enough relevant, labelled data (or a way to get it)? Could simple rules, a lookup or basic analytics solve it well enough? Can the organisation tolerate errors, and act on probabilistic outputs? Can it be maintained — pipelines, monitoring, retraining, ownership? If a heuristic gets most of the benefit, I'd ship that first and use it as the baseline. Saying “this doesn't need ML yet” is a perfectly good — and senior — answer.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-d5c871b", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "98. You find a serious bug in a model that has been live for three months. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Contain, then communicate, then learn. Assess the blast radius quickly: which predictions were affected, since when, with what consequence for customers and money. Mitigate at once — roll back, patch, or switch to a fallback. Tell stakeholders promptly and plainly: what happened, the impact, what's been done, what's next; don't wait for a perfect analysis. Decide with the business whether past decisions need correcting (re-scoring, customer remediation, regulatory notification). Then a blameless post-mortem: root cause, why tests and monitoring missed it, and concrete preventive actions — a test that reproduces the bug, a new monitor, a review step.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-97f6804", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "99. How do you estimate the business value of a model before building it?", "a": "<p>Build a back-of-envelope value model. Start from the decision it changes and volumes: for example, 1,000,000 transactions a month, 0.2% fraud at ₹5,000 average loss = ₹1 crore of fraud. If the current rules catch 50% and a model could plausibly catch 65%, that's ₹15 lakh a month gained, minus the cost of extra false alarms (review time, customer friction) and of building and running the system. Give a range (pessimistic/expected/optimistic), name the assumptions that matter most, and propose the cheapest experiment to test them — a quick offline prototype against historical data — before committing a team.</p>"}, {"id": "situational-131064d", "p": "situational.html", "g": "100 situational questions", "grp": "Practice", "l": 2, "q": "100. Tell me about a time a model you built failed. (How should you structure the answer?)", "a": "<p>Interviewers are checking ownership and learning, not perfection. Use STAR with the weight on the last two parts:</p><ol><li><b>Situation/Task:</b> the model, its purpose, and what “failed” meant in numbers.</li><li><b>Action:</b> how you detected it, how you diagnosed it (what you ruled out, what the root cause was — leakage, drift, skew, wrong metric), and what you did in the short term.</li><li><b>Result:</b> the measurable outcome after the fix.</li><li><b>Lesson:</b> what you changed permanently — a validation scheme that mirrors production, a monitor, a pre-launch checklist.</li></ol><p>Pick a real technical failure, own your part without blaming others, and avoid “failures” that are disguised successes.</p>"}];
